// GCSE Geography model answers (AQA 8035, Edexcel B 1GB0, OCR B J384, Eduqas B C112).
// Written from each paper's official mark scheme. Keys are "<paperId>::<partCode>".
window.GEOG_GCSE_MODEL_ANSWERS = {
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::01.1": "D — Oil spill. It is caused by human activity, whereas tsunamis, earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are natural processes.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::01.2": "C — Hurricane Dorian passed close to the east coast of the USA.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::01.3": "About 2,900 km. The hurricane-force section starts near the Virgin Islands and runs north-west to The Bahamas, then north along the US east coast; measured against the 1000 km scale bar it is just under three scale-lengths.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::01.4": "1. It has a central eye — a small clear area with no cloud in the middle of the storm.\n2. It has dense spiralling bands of cloud rotating anticlockwise around the eye, giving a roughly circular shape about 600–800 km across.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::01.5": "Draw the 2018 bar to a total height of 15. Shade the lower part (0–13) with the diagonal hatching used for tropical storms and hurricanes scale 1–2, and the upper part (13–15) in grey for hurricanes scale 3 and above.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::01.6": "Rising sea surface temperatures caused by climate change mean that more of the ocean is above about 27 °C, so more storms can form.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::01.7": "Evacuation plans and evacuation centres mean that people know where to go to be safe when a storm is forecast, which reduces the number of deaths and injuries.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::01.8": "I partly agree. Figure 4 suggests that two types of extreme event are becoming more common: moorland fires, linked to higher summer temperatures and lower rainfall, and flooding, linked to higher rainfall and more storms. Recent events support this — the Somerset Levels flooding in 2014, Storms Ciara and Dennis in 2020, the \"Beast from the East\" cold spell in 2018 and the record 40.3 °C heatwave in 2022. Met Office data show that UK temperatures have risen by about 1 °C since 1980 and that more winter rain now falls in heavy events, which increases the frequency of floods and droughts, and this fits with global climate change.\n\nHowever, UK weather has always been very variable, and a few dramatic events are not proof of a long-term trend; records only go back around 150 years, and media coverage makes recent events seem more frequent. Many parts of the UK have not experienced significantly different weather. Overall, the evidence for more extreme temperatures and heavy rainfall is strong, but the trend for other types of weather such as storms is less certain, so UK weather does seem to be becoming more extreme in some ways.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::01.9": "At a destructive margin an oceanic plate and a continental plate move towards each other. The denser oceanic plate is forced down (subducted) beneath the lighter continental plate into the mantle. Friction between the plates causes pressure to build up; when the rocks finally slip, the stored energy is released as an earthquake. As the oceanic plate descends it heats up and melts, forming magma. This magma is less dense than the surrounding rock, so it rises through cracks in the continental crust and erupts at the surface, forming volcanoes such as those in the Andes.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::01.10": "The effects of tectonic hazards do vary a lot between areas of contrasting wealth, but wealth is not the only factor.\n\nThe Nepal (Gorkha) earthquake of 2015, in one of Asia's poorest countries, killed over 8,000 people, injured over 20,000 and left more than a million people homeless. Most rural homes were made of mud brick or unmortared stone and collapsed; landslides blocked roads so aid took weeks to reach remote villages, and an avalanche killed 19 people at Everest Base Camp. Tourism, a key source of income, collapsed and rebuilding was still incomplete five years later. By contrast, the L'Aquila earthquake in Italy (2009), a wealthier country, killed about 300 people and made 60,000 homeless. Emergency services responded within hours, a state of emergency was declared, and most people were rehoused in temporary housing within months.\n\nWealth explains much of this difference: richer countries can afford earthquake-resistant buildings, monitoring, emergency drills and rapid response, and they recover faster because of insurance and government funds. Poorer countries rely on international aid, which can be slow. However, Nepal's earthquake was much stronger (magnitude 7.8 compared with 6.3, roughly 31 times more powerful) and struck a mountainous region where access was very difficult, so physical factors also mattered. Even wealthy Japan suffered over 15,000 deaths in 2011 because the tsunami overwhelmed its defences.\n\nOverall, wealth strongly influences the scale of deaths, homelessness and the speed of recovery, but the magnitude of the hazard, the terrain and the time of day can be just as important, so effects vary to a large but not complete extent with wealth.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::02.1": "D — Coniferous forests occur in large areas of North America, Europe and Asia.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::02.2": "Africa.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::02.3": "B — The trees drop their dead leaves because of lower temperatures in winter.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::02.4": "Figure 6 shows that Iquitos is hot all year (about 26–27 °C) with very high rainfall in every month (from about 150 mm in August to 350 mm in March, over 2,500 mm in total), so there is no winter and plants can grow all year round. This creates intense competition for sunlight. Trees grow very tall with straight, branchless trunks so their crowns can reach the light in the canopy or emerge above it. Because the trees are so tall and the soils are shallow and nutrient-poor, they develop large buttress roots, like those in the photograph, to support them. Climbing plants such as lianas use the trees to climb towards the light, and epiphytes grow on branches high in the canopy.\n\nThe heavy daily rainfall means leaves have waxy surfaces and drip tips so water runs off quickly, preventing the growth of algae and stopping branches breaking under the weight of water. Bark is thin and smooth because there is no cold season to protect against, and it lets rainwater run off. Leaves are shed gradually throughout the year rather than in one season. On the dark forest floor, which receives only about 2% of sunlight, plants such as the ferns in the photograph have large leaves to capture as much light as possible.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::02.5": "From 2002 to 2015 forest loss fluctuated but stayed fairly constant at around 2.5–3.6 million hectares a year. It then rose sharply in 2016, roughly doubling to about 6.1 million hectares, before falling to 3.7 million hectares by 2018.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::02.6": "Governments have introduced stricter laws and monitoring to stop illegal logging.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::02.7": "Removing the trees has left the soil bare, and heavy rain has washed it away, forming gullies, so soil erosion is taking place.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::02.8": "Ecotourism is small-scale tourism that employs local people as guides and uses local produce, so the community earns money from keeping the forest standing rather than cutting it down. Visitor numbers are limited and some of the profits are used for conservation, so the rainforest is protected for the future.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::02.9": "Hot desert environment (Figure 9) — case study: the Western Desert of Australia.\n\nThe Western Desert offers important opportunities for development. Figure 9 shows a large gold mine in Western Australia, and the region also has iron ore, nickel and other minerals; mining earns billions of dollars in exports and creates well-paid jobs, and the multiplier effect supports local services and transport. The very high sunshine levels give opportunities for solar energy. Tourism is growing, attracting visitors to landscapes such as Uluru and Aboriginal cultural sites, which brings income to local communities. Irrigated farming is possible in some areas.\n\nHowever, there are major challenges. Figure 9 shows a barren, rocky landscape with sparse vegetation and no visible roads or settlements, showing how remote and inaccessible the area is. Temperatures can exceed 40 °C by day and fall below freezing at night, making outdoor work dangerous and increasing costs. Rainfall is low and unreliable, so water supply for workers, mines and farming is a serious problem, and supplying water through long pipelines is expensive. Infrastructure must be built over huge distances, workers often have to be flown in and out, and development can conflict with Aboriginal land rights and damage fragile ecosystems.\n\nOverall, the desert offers valuable opportunities, especially for mining and solar power, but these can only be exploited where the returns are high enough to overcome the costs of extreme heat, water shortage and remoteness. Large-scale mining like that in Figure 9 is worthwhile because gold is so valuable, whereas smaller-scale development is much harder.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::03.1": "B — 2.0–2.9 metres per year.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::03.2": "The rate of erosion generally increases from north to south, from about 1.7 m to over 5 m per year, before dropping at the southernmost site.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::03.3": "C — Spit.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::03.4": "Flamborough Head is made of chalk, which is harder and more resistant than the soft boulder clay to the south, so it is eroded more slowly and sticks out into the sea.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::03.5": "The cliffs are made of soft boulder clay that is easily eroded and prone to slumping.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::03.6": "Waves attack the base of the cliff between high and low tide through hydraulic action and abrasion. This erosion forms a wave-cut notch at the cliff foot. As the notch gets deeper, the rock above it is left overhanging and unsupported, and eventually it collapses. Waves remove the fallen debris and the process starts again, so the cliff gradually retreats inland. The gently sloping rocky surface left behind at the base of the old cliff is the wave-cut platform, which is exposed at low tide and smoothed by abrasion.\n\nDiagram (described): three cross-sections showing (1) a cliff with a notch at high-tide level, (2) the overhang collapsing, (3) the cliff further inland with a wide, gently sloping platform between high and low water marks.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::03.7": "Figure 13 shows two rock groynes. The north groyne has trapped sediment moved by longshore drift, building a wider beach that absorbs wave energy and protects the cliffs; groynes are relatively cheap and can last up to 40 years, and wider beaches benefit tourism. However, the cliff immediately south of the south groyne is collapsing, because the groynes starve the downdrift coast of sediment, so the problem is moved along the coast — they are effective locally but not for the whole coastline.\n\nRock armour has been placed along the foot of the cliff. It absorbs and dissipates wave energy, is quick to build and is much cheaper than a sea wall. Combined with regrading the cliff to a gentler, more stable angle, it protects the settlement on the clifftop. However, recent slumping behind the rock armour shows that it cannot stop mass movement caused by rainwater saturating the clay, so it is only partly effective.\n\nOther strategies include sea walls, which are very effective at stopping erosion and flooding but cost several thousand pounds per metre and can be undercut, and soft engineering such as beach nourishment, which is cheaper and more natural but needs regular repeating. Overall, a combination of hard and soft engineering, as in Figure 13, is effective at protecting high-value places, but it is expensive and can increase erosion elsewhere, so it is not effective for the coastline as a whole.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::04.1": "Plot a cross at 66 km on the horizontal axis and 9.0 m on the vertical axis — between the plots for site 5 (57 km, 10.4 m) and site 7 (78 km, 14.6 m).",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::04.2": "The river gets wider downstream, from 1.9 m near the source to 14.6 m at 78 km — almost eight times wider.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::04.3": "Sediment is broken down by attrition as particles collide with each other while they are transported, making them smaller downstream.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::04.4": "A — Interlocking spurs.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::04.5": "The valley sides are steep, giving a V-shaped valley.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::04.6": "Meanders form where water flows faster on one side of the channel than the other. On the outside of a bend the water is deeper and flows fastest with less friction, so lateral erosion by hydraulic action and abrasion undercuts the bank, forming a steep river cliff. On the inside of the bend the water is shallow and slow, with more friction, so the river loses energy and deposits sediment, building a gently sloping slip-off slope. Because erosion continues on the outer bank and deposition on the inner bank, the bend becomes more pronounced and the meander migrates across the floodplain and downstream over time.\n\nDiagram (described): a plan view of a bend with an arrow for the fastest flow swinging to the outside, labelled \"river cliff — erosion\" on the outer bank and \"slip-off slope — deposition\" on the inner bank, and a cross-section showing a deep, steep outer side and a shallow, gently sloping inner side.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::04.7": "Hard engineering, which the local resident in Figure 16 wants, can give a high level of protection to homes on the floodplain. Dams and reservoirs store floodwater and release it slowly, and can also provide drinking water and hydroelectric power; flood walls and embankments increase the capacity of the channel so the river overflows less often. These are effective for protecting high-value property such as towns, railways and water treatment works, as at Banbury, where an embankment created a flood storage area protecting hundreds of homes. However, they cost millions of pounds, need maintenance, can damage habitats, and may move flooding downstream.\n\nSoft engineering, favoured by the Environment Officer, works with natural processes. Planting trees in the catchment increases interception and infiltration, slowing the flow of water to the river, while also storing carbon and creating habitats. River restoration, such as removing straightened channels and reconnecting the floodplain, stores floodwater where it does less harm. Flood warnings and floodplain zoning reduce the damage when floods do occur. These methods are much cheaper, need little maintenance and are more sustainable, but they take time to work and may not protect against very large floods.\n\nOverall, hard engineering offers the most reliable protection for people already living on floodplains, but soft engineering has greater long-term benefits for the environment and costs less, so the best approach often combines both.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::05.1": "Continue the line from about 2,000 m and draw it falling steeply down to 0 m at Y, showing the ice thinning rapidly towards the edge of the ice sheet.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::05.2": "The ice was much deeper over Scandinavia — over 2,500 m compared with just over 1,500 m over the British Isles, about 1,000 m deeper.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::05.3": "C — The ice sheet extended westwards from Scandinavia to the British Isles.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::05.4": "A — Moraine.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::05.5": "The ice transports material of all sizes together and deposits it without sorting it when it melts.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::05.6": "A glacier forms when ice from corries flows down into an existing V-shaped river valley. The glacier is very heavy and moves downhill, eroding the valley by abrasion — rocks frozen into the base and sides of the ice scrape the bedrock like sandpaper — and by plucking, where meltwater freezes onto the rock and pulls loose blocks away as the ice moves. Because the glacier fills the whole valley, it erodes the floor and the sides, widening and deepening the valley and cutting off the ends of the interlocking spurs to form truncated spurs. When the ice melts, a steep-sided, flat-floored U-shaped glacial trough remains, often with a ribbon lake in an over-deepened section and a misfit river on its floor.\n\nDiagram (described): cross-sections showing (1) a V-shaped river valley, (2) the valley filled with a glacier eroding the sides and floor, (3) the resulting U-shaped valley with steep sides, flat floor and truncated spurs.",
  "geog-aqa-8035-2022-p1::05.7": "Figure 20 shows that tourism in Snowdonia is growing — 3.89 million visitors in 2015, up from about 3.6 million in 2013 — and each visitor spends about £122. This brings large economic benefits: money is spent in shops, cafés, hotels, campsites and on outdoor activities, creating jobs and supporting local services such as buses and village shops through the multiplier effect. Farmers can diversify into B&B and campsites. However, many jobs are seasonal and low-paid, most visitors are day visitors (2.43 million) who spend less, and demand for second homes raises house prices so local people struggle to buy homes.\n\nThe environmental impacts are mainly negative. With 595,000 people visiting Snowdon every year, popular paths suffer from footpath erosion, vegetation is trampled and erosion scars form. Crowds at the summit, shown in the photograph, create litter, and traffic causes congestion, air pollution and parking on verges. Walkers and dogs can disturb sheep and wildlife. On the other hand, some tourist income is used for conservation and footpath repair by the National Park Authority.\n\nOverall, tourism brings significant economic benefits that the local economy depends on, but these come at an environmental cost that is concentrated at honeypot sites like Snowdon; with good management the economic benefits can outweigh the environmental damage.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::1(a)(i)": "By 2100 none of the largest cities are in North America or South America, whereas in 2050 there are several (e.g. New York, Mexico City, São Paulo). Many more of the largest cities will be in Africa, such as Kinshasa, Lagos, Niamey and Lusaka, and more of them will be south of the Equator. There will be fewer in Asia — East Asian cities such as Tokyo and Shanghai drop out — so the largest cities become concentrated in Africa and South Asia.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::1(a)(ii)": "24 − 15 = 9 million. 9 ÷ 15 × 100 = 60% increase.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::1(a)(iii)": "1. Rural–urban migration: people move from the countryside because of poverty, few jobs and poor schools and healthcare, attracted by the chance of better-paid work in factories and services in the city.\n2. Natural increase: many migrants are young adults, so there are many births, and better healthcare in cities means people live longer, so birth rates are much higher than death rates.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::1(b)": "Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. Around 1.5 million people live in favelas such as Rocinha. The Favela-Bairro project upgraded many favelas rather than demolishing them: residents were given legal ownership of their land, roads were paved, and water supply, sewers and street lighting were installed. Self-help schemes provided materials such as bricks so residents could replace wooden shacks with brick homes, and community centres and crèches were built. In Complexo do Alemão a cable car improved residents' access to jobs, and some families were rehoused in new apartment blocks in the Minha Casa Minha Vida programme.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::1(c)(i)": "Exports; imports; tariffs; trade blocs.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::1(c)(ii)": "Draw a line from the centre to divide the blank 71% into two sectors: Coffee 50% (180°) and Tea 21% (75.6°). Shade each sector using the shading shown for coffee and tea in the key.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::1(c)(iii)": "Fair trade means producers get a fair, guaranteed price for goods such as coffee, so they earn a steady income and can afford food, healthcare and education for their families. The extra Fairtrade premium is spent on community projects such as clean water supplies or schools, which improves health and gives children better job opportunities, reducing the gap with richer countries.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::1(d)": "I mostly agree, but the balance depends on how well the host country manages the MNC.\n\nMNCs like Unilever bring important advantages. Unilever employs thousands of people in its factories, offices and supply chains in countries such as China, Thailand and Sri Lanka, providing more reliable and often higher incomes than local alternatives. These workers spend their wages in local shops and businesses, creating a positive multiplier effect. MNCs pay taxes which governments can spend on health and education, and they often improve infrastructure such as roads, ports and power supplies. They also bring new skills and technology.\n\nHowever, there are disadvantages. Wages in some MNC factories are very low and workers may have few rights; some MNCs have been linked to sweatshops and child labour. Most profits are sent back to the MNC's home country rather than reinvested locally. Factories can cause water and air pollution, and because MNCs are footloose they may close a factory and move to a cheaper country, causing unemployment and a negative multiplier effect.\n\nOverall, I agree that MNCs bring more advantages than disadvantages, especially for emerging economies that need investment and jobs, but only where governments enforce environmental and labour laws to reduce the disadvantages.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::2(a)(i)": "Top: saltation. Middle: suspension. Bottom: traction.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::2(a)(ii)": "A spit.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::2(a)(iii)": "Soft, less resistant rock such as clay is eroded much more quickly by the waves than hard rock such as granite.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::2(b)(i)": "In the UK, rising sea levels and stormier weather caused by climate change are increasing coastal erosion and flooding. On the Holderness coast the soft clay cliffs are retreating by up to 2 m a year, and villages such as Skipsea are losing homes, roads and farmland; people often receive no compensation. In low-lying areas such as parts of Norfolk and the Thames estuary, more frequent storm surges increase the risk of flooding, so more money must be spent on defences such as the Thames Barrier. Some communities may eventually have to be relocated, and coastal tourism businesses may lose income.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::2(b)(ii)": "1. It is very expensive — sea walls and rock armour cost millions of pounds to build and need regular maintenance, money that could be spent on schools or hospitals.\n2. Defences can increase erosion further along the coast because they stop sediment being moved by longshore drift, so neighbouring communities lose their beaches.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::2(c)(i)": "Urban areas are covered in impermeable surfaces such as tarmac and concrete, so rain cannot infiltrate and instead runs quickly over the surface and through drains into rivers, raising river levels rapidly.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::2(c)(ii)": "(1) False. (2) True. (3) False. (4) True.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::2(c)(iii)": "Draw a bar for schools up to 35 and a bar for care homes up to 2, the same width as the existing bars.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::2(c)(iv)": "1. A pie chart. 2. A pictogram (or a divided bar graph).",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::2(d)(i)": "The scheme works with natural processes rather than building large concrete defences. The sponge roads let rainwater soak through instead of running off, reducing surface run-off and the risk of flooding. The water is stored underground in tunnels and tanks, so it has little visual impact and could be reused in times of drought. Planting vegetation beside the roads absorbs water and creates habitats for wildlife, improving biodiversity. The scheme is relatively cheap (about £6 million) and will help Slough cope with heavier rainfall expected with climate change, so it meets present needs without harming future generations.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::2(d)(ii)": "Area B is the commercial centre of Slough, so a flood would have serious economic consequences. The works beside the canal at 983801 and 988802 could have equipment and stock damaged and lose production, costing businesses money and putting workers' jobs at risk. Shops and offices in the town centre would have to close, losing trade, and the museum (977799) and motel (987797) would lose income from visitors. Flooding of the railway line and Slough station, the bus station and main roads such as the A4, A412 and B416 would stop people getting to work across the area, affecting businesses beyond Area B. Large areas of terraced housing in Upton would be damaged, leading to costly repairs and high insurance claims.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::3(a)(i)": "Hot semi-arid grasslands are found between the Tropic of Cancer and the Tropic of Capricorn. The largest areas are in Africa, in a belt south of the Sahara and across southern Africa. There are also areas in northern and eastern South America and northern Australia, and a small area in India. There are none in Europe.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::3(a)(ii)": "Acacia trees have long tap roots that reach deep groundwater during the long dry season, and small waxy leaves that reduce water loss through transpiration. Baobab trees store water in their large barrel-shaped trunks (up to 500 litres) and have thick, fire-resistant bark, which protects them from the frequent grass fires.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::3(a)(iii)": "The variety of living things in an ecosystem.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::3(a)(iv)": "In the Amazon tropical rainforest, human activity has greatly reduced biodiversity. Large areas have been cleared for cattle ranching and soya farming, and logging and road building break the forest into fragments. This destroys the habitats of species such as jaguars and many birds and insects, some of which are found nowhere else, so species become endangered or extinct. However, some human activity protects biodiversity: national parks, indigenous reserves and ecotourism projects help to conserve habitats.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::3(b)": "Epping Forest in Essex is a small-scale woodland ecosystem of about 2,400 hectares. It is dominated by deciduous trees such as oak, beech and hornbeam, many of them ancient pollarded trees, with a shrub layer of holly and a field layer of bluebells and bracken. It supports wildlife such as deer, badgers and stag beetles, and decomposers such as fungi recycle nutrients from the leaf litter. It also has visitor facilities, including paths and visitor centres.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::3(c)(i)": "Oceania; 2000; 500; Asia.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::3(c)(ii)": "The data is continuous and shows how water consumption has changed over time.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::3(c)(iii)": "As the population grows, more water is needed for domestic use such as drinking, cooking, showers and toilets. More food has to be grown to feed the extra people, and much of this needs irrigation, which uses about 70% of freshwater. More houses with gardens and more leisure facilities such as swimming pools also increase demand, and more electricity must be generated, which uses water for cooling or needs new reservoirs.",
  "geog-eduqas-c112-2024-c1::3(d)": "In some ways a scheme like Itaipu would be a good model for other countries. It is a multipurpose scheme that supplies 90% of Paraguay's electricity and 16% of Brazil's from renewable hydroelectric power, meeting growing demand for both water and energy without burning fossil fuels. It has tried to reduce its environmental impact: over 44 million trees were planted, creating habitats for endangered species such as the jaguar, the forest reduces sedimentation of the reservoir, and 1,200 farms use organic methods. Involving local communities gave them a sense of ownership, and its status as a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve shows it is internationally recognised as sustainable.\n\nHowever, the scheme may not be suitable everywhere. It needed cooperation between Brazil and Paraguay, which is difficult to achieve in other regions where rivers cross borders and can cause conflict, as with the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam on the Nile. Large dams are extremely expensive and need suitable physical geography — a large river and a deep valley — which many countries lack. Building Itaipu also destroyed large areas of forest and displaced people; copying this elsewhere could add to climate change and harm communities. Smaller, cheaper schemes such as rainwater harvesting may be more appropriate for poorer countries.\n\nOverall, I think projects like Itaipu are a good option for countries with large rivers, enough money and good international relations, but they should not automatically be seen as the best option everywhere; small-scale, local schemes may be more sustainable in many places.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::1(a)(i)": "A — Cairo.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::1(a)(ii)": "2883 − 18 = 2865 mm.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::1(b)": "Areas of low pressure, such as near the Equator, have high rainfall. Here the ground is heated strongly so warm air rises; as it rises it cools, and the water vapour condenses into towering clouds, producing heavy convectional rain.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::1(c)(i)": "A conservative (transform) plate boundary.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::1(c)(ii)": "The two plates try to slide past each other but friction makes them stick, so stress builds up. When the stress overcomes the friction, the plates suddenly jerk past each other, releasing energy as an earthquake.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::1(c)(iii)": "Slab pull: where dense oceanic crust is subducted at a destructive boundary, it sinks into the mantle under gravity and pulls the rest of the plate along behind it.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::1(d)(i)": "C — Tehran.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::1(d)(ii)": "1554 − 1286 = 268 mm. 268 ÷ 1554 × 100 = 17.2% decrease.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::1(d)(iii)": "1. Rising air temperatures melt glaciers and ice sheets on land, such as in Greenland and Antarctica, adding extra water to the oceans.\n2. As the oceans warm, seawater expands (thermal expansion), so the same mass of water takes up more space and sea level rises.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::1(e)": "1. Volcanic eruptions release ash and sulphur dioxide high into the atmosphere, which reflect incoming solar radiation, causing global temperatures to fall for a few years.\n2. Milankovitch cycles — changes in the shape of the Earth's orbit, its tilt and its wobble — alter the amount of solar energy reaching the Earth, causing glacial and interglacial periods over thousands of years.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::1(f)": "Tropical cyclones produce several physical hazards: storm surges and coastal flooding, very strong winds, intense rainfall causing river flooding, and landslides.\n\nCoastal flooding is often the most deadly hazard. The very low pressure and strong onshore winds pile water up against the coast, producing a storm surge. During Hurricane Katrina in 2005 a surge of over 7 m overwhelmed the levees in New Orleans, flooding about 80% of the city, and in Typhoon Haiyan (2013) a 5–6 m surge swept through Tacloban, causing most of the 6,300 deaths. Coastal flooding also contaminates farmland and water supplies with salt and erodes coastlines, and rising sea levels will make it worse.\n\nHowever, other hazards can be just as important. Strong winds of up to 185 mph during Hurricane Dorian in 2019 destroyed most wooden buildings on Grand Bahama and brought down power lines, hampering the relief effort. Heavy rainfall can cause inland river flooding, sewage overflows and disease, and on steep, deforested slopes such as in the Philippines it triggers deadly landslides, which can affect areas far from the coast that a surge never reaches.\n\nThe importance of each hazard depends on local geography and human factors: New Orleans was badly affected by coastal flooding because it lies below sea level and its levees failed, whereas in mountainous areas landslides may cause more damage. Overall, coastal flooding is usually the main hazard because it affects densely populated, low-lying coastal areas and causes the most deaths, but in some places wind, rainfall and landslides are more significant, so it is not always the main hazard.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::2(a)(i)": "B — life expectancy.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::2(a)(ii)": "Maternal mortality rates are much higher in developing countries because there are fewer hospitals and trained midwives to help with complications during childbirth.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::2(b)(i)": "135 + 135 + 129 + 125 + 124 + 120 + 116 = 884. 884 ÷ 7 = 126.28…, so the mean is 126.3 PM2.5.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::2(b)(ii)": "20 : 120 = 1 : 6.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::2(c)": "As a country becomes richer, its government can afford to introduce and enforce environmental laws, such as limits on factory emissions, which reduce air pollution.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::2(d)(i)": "14.21 − 0.4 = US$13.81 trillion.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::2(d)(ii)": "A comparative (paired) bar chart, with each country along the x-axis and GDP in trillions of US$ on the y-axis, and two bars for each country — one for 1990 and one for 2019 — in different colours.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::2(e)": "In India, improvements in broadband and satellite communications allowed companies to provide IT and business services to customers around the world in real time. This attracted foreign companies to outsource call centres and software development to cities such as Bengaluru, creating millions of well-paid jobs and increasing export earnings.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::2(f)(i)": "D — 0–4.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::2(f)(ii)": "The pyramid has a very wide base, with over 15% of the population aged 0–4. This shows a high birth rate, which is typical of a developing country where access to contraception and female education is limited and children are needed to help support the family.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::2(g)": "1. India has a long coastline on the Arabian Sea and Bay of Bengal, with major ports such as Mumbai and Chennai, which makes it easy to import raw materials and export manufactured goods, encouraging trade.\n2. India is 5½ hours ahead of the UK, so Indian companies can work on tasks while it is night-time in Europe, making it an attractive location for outsourcing IT and call centres that need 24-hour operation.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::2(h)": "Colonialism was very important in creating global inequality. European powers took control of large parts of Africa, Asia and South America and organised their economies to supply cheap raw materials such as cotton, cocoa and minerals to the colonial power, while importing expensive manufactured goods. Infrastructure was built to move resources to ports rather than to develop the colony, and local industries were often destroyed. This left many countries at independence with economies dependent on low-value primary exports, which Frank's dependency theory argues kept them in a \"periphery\" serving the developed \"core\".\n\nNeo-colonialism has continued this. TNCs from developed countries operate in developing countries, but most profits are repatriated to their headquarters; trade rules and subsidies in developed countries make it hard for poorer countries to export processed goods; and debt repayments take money away from health and education. Chinese investment in African infrastructure in return for raw materials may be reinforcing dependence on commodity exports.\n\nHowever, other factors also matter. Physical factors such as being landlocked, a harsh climate, disease and natural hazards make development harder. Poor governance and corruption can waste resources — some former colonies with valuable resources remain poor because of conflict and corruption — while countries such as South Korea and Singapore, also once colonised, have developed rapidly.\n\nOverall, colonialism laid the foundations of global inequality and neo-colonialism continues to widen it, so historical factors are very important, but they do not fully explain why some countries develop and others do not; governance and physical factors are also significant.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::3(a)(i)": "Asia (50%).",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::3(a)(ii)": "38% − 9% = a decrease of 29 percentage points.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::3(a)(iii)": "Africa's projected share is 20%. 6.5 billion × 0.2 = 1.3 billion people.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::3(b)": "Deindustrialisation — when factories and docks closed, many people lost their jobs and moved away from the city to find work elsewhere, so the population fell.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::3(c)": "The blank 60% of the circle is divided into three segments: Nepal 15% (54°), India 25% (90°) and Pakistan 20% (72°). Starting from the end of the Iran segment, draw a line 54° round for Nepal, then a line a further 90° round for India; the remaining 72° is Pakistan. Label each segment.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::3(d)": "Regeneration can increase the urban population, because converting old industrial buildings into flats and creating new jobs attracts people to move back into the area.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::3(e)(i)": "B — Seoul.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::3(e)(ii)": "There is a positive correlation: the higher the percentage of the national population living in the capital, the greater the capital's contribution to national GDP. For example, Washington D.C. has under 1% of the US population and contributes only about 1% of GDP, while Athens has about 28% of Greece's population and contributes about 48% of GDP. Ottawa is an anomaly — it has only about 4% of Canada's population but contributes about 39% of GDP.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::3(f)": "C — a city with a very large economic and political influence.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::3(g)": "Formal employment is officially registered, so workers have a contract, pay tax and are protected by laws on minimum wages and safety, whereas informal employment, such as street selling, is unregistered, so workers pay no tax but have no job security or legal protection.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::3(h)": "1. Bottom-up projects are low-cost — for example, community toilet blocks built by SPARC and Mahila Milan in Mumbai — so even the poorest slum dwellers can benefit, improving health by reducing open defecation.\n2. Local people are involved in planning and running the project, so it tackles the problems they think are most important and they are more likely to maintain it in the long term, using their own skills.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p1::3(i)": "Mumbai's population has grown from about 3 million in 1950 to over 20 million in its metropolitan area today.\n\nIn the past, growth was driven by Mumbai's role as a colonial port and its textile mills, which attracted workers from across India. After independence, industrialisation, the port and the growth of finance and the Bollywood film industry made it India's richest city. Rural–urban migration was the most important factor: push factors in poorer states such as Bihar and Uttar Pradesh — poverty, lack of land, drought and few services — combined with pull factors of jobs, higher wages and better services in Mumbai. In 2010 about 75% of Mumbai's migrants came from rural areas of India and only about 2% from abroad, showing that national rather than international migration matters most.\n\nToday, the rate of migration is slowing as other cities grow and Mumbai becomes very expensive, so natural increase is becoming relatively more important: many migrants are young adults, so birth rates in the city are higher than death rates. Government investment in infrastructure, such as new metro lines and the Mumbai Trans Harbour Link, attracts more investment and people through the multiplier effect.\n\nOverall, rural–urban migration has been the most important cause of Mumbai's growth in the past, while in recent years natural increase and government-led economic growth have become more significant as migration slows.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::1(a)": "B — North-west Scotland.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::1(b)": "1. Farming has cleared most of the original forest cover in lowland England, creating a distinctive patchwork landscape of fields separated by hedgerows, whereas upland areas used for sheep grazing remain open moorland.\n2. Building settlements has replaced natural land use with urban landscapes of buildings and roads, and managing rivers and coasts with defences such as embankments and sea walls has made these areas much less natural.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::2(a)(i)": "C — from the north-west.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::2(a)(ii)": "Higher waves carry more energy, so they erode the coast more quickly through hydraulic action and abrasion.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::2(a)(iii)": "The prevailing wind makes waves approach the coast at an angle. The swash carries sediment up the beach at that angle, but the backwash returns it straight down the slope under gravity. This zigzag movement — longshore drift — transports sediment along the coast in the direction of the waves. Where the coastline changes direction, for example at a river mouth, the sediment continues to be carried forward and is deposited in the sea, gradually building a spit. So the wave direction controls the direction in which sediment moves and therefore where the spit forms and grows.\n\nDiagram (described): a plan view of a beach with arrows showing swash at an angle and backwash at right angles, a zigzag arrow along the beach labelled longshore drift, and a spit extending across a river mouth where the coast changes direction.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::3(a)": "Trees intercept rainfall, so it takes longer for water to reach the river, reducing the peak discharge.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::3(b)": "Hard engineering such as flood walls is strong and reliable, so it protects homes and businesses from flood damage that would otherwise cost millions of pounds.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::3(c)": "River Tees. Sediment load increases downstream because erosion adds material and tributaries bring in more sediment, but the particles get smaller and rounder because of attrition, so more is carried in suspension in the lower course. Velocity generally increases downstream even though the gradient is gentler, because the channel becomes wider, deeper and smoother, so there is less friction between the water and the bed and banks — the channel is more efficient.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::4": "At a national scale, woodland is unevenly distributed: only 13% of the UK is wooded, varying from 10% in England to 18% in Scotland. A key reason is economics — forestry gives low profits compared with farming and other land uses, so in fertile lowland England landowners choose farming, housing or industry instead. In Scotland, large areas of upland are poor for farming, so plantations of evergreens are one of the few profitable uses, which is why Scotland has more woodland. However, much Scottish upland is also kept treeless for grouse moors and deer stalking.\n\nAt a local scale, Figure 2 shows a close link between woodland and relief around Fort William. There is no woodland on the highest ground in the south-east, such as Ben Nevis (1,345 m) and Aonach Mòr (1,221 m), because conditions there are too cold, wet and windy and soils too thin for trees. Woodland is found on the lower slopes and valley sides, such as Leanachan Forest, where it is sheltered and the soil is deeper. The straight edges suggest it is plantation forestry. Remote, steep areas without roads are expensive to harvest, so they are less likely to be planted.\n\nOverall, physical factors such as altitude, climate and soil explain the local pattern, but economic factors — the low profitability of forestry compared with farming — are the most important reason for the national pattern. As the UK imports so much timber, rising prices may encourage more planting in future.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::5(a)(i)": "D — London.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::5(a)(ii)": "Ordered values: 15.5, 18.4, 18.4, 21.1, 21.5, 22.6, 23.9, 24.3, 24.4. The median (5th value) is a 21.5% fall.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::5(b)": "1. It has increased the population unevenly — most international migrants settle where there are most jobs, especially London and other large cities, so these grow faster than rural areas.\n2. It has made the population younger and more ethnically diverse, because most migrants are young adults coming to work or study, and they often have children in the UK, especially in cities such as London, Birmingham and Leicester.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::6(a)(i)": "Milton Keynes has a younger population structure than England and Wales. It has a larger proportion of people aged 25–44 (about 4% in each 5-year group compared with about 3.3%) and more young children aged 0–4, but noticeably fewer people in all age groups over 55 — for example about 1.7% of males aged 65–69 compared with 2.5% in England and Wales.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::6(a)(ii)": "554,500 − 462,400 = 92,100. 92,100 ÷ 462,400 × 100 = 19.9%.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::6(b)": "London. Site: London grew at the lowest bridging point of the River Thames and at the point where the river was still tidal, so ships could reach it; this made it a nodal point where routes met and markets developed, leading to a multiplier effect. Situation: London is in south-east England, close to mainland Europe across the North Sea and English Channel, which encouraged trade, and it was the centre of the British Empire's trade, making it a global port and financial centre.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::6(c)": "1. Deindustrialisation in East London, when the docks closed, led to job losses and low incomes in boroughs such as Tower Hamlets and Newham, so these areas still have higher deprivation and poorer health than wealthy boroughs such as Kensington and Chelsea.\n2. Very high house prices mean wealthy people concentrate in expensive areas, while lower-income households live in areas of social housing, so incomes, education results and life expectancy vary greatly between different parts of the city.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::7": "Figure 6 shows that the five unhealthiest high streets — Blackpool, Grimsby, Sunderland, Stoke-on-Trent and Walsall — are all in areas of below-average life expectancy, mainly in the north and Midlands, whereas the south generally has better life expectancy. Three are coastal towns.\n\nThe main cause of these variations is probably deprivation. Many of these towns were hit by deindustrialisation (coal, steel, fishing, pottery) or the decline of seaside tourism, leading to unemployment and low incomes. Businesses such as payday lenders, bookmakers and fast-food outlets locate where rents are low and where there is demand from people on low incomes, while healthier outlets such as gyms and galleries close. So unhealthy high streets are as much a consequence of deprivation as a cause of it. National trends such as online shopping are also emptying high streets everywhere, even in wealthier areas.\n\nThe consequences are serious. Easy access to fast food, gambling and high-interest loans may encourage unhealthy lifestyles and debt, contributing to lower life expectancy and creating a cycle of deprivation. Declining high streets lose shoppers and jobs, and the areas become less attractive to investors. Politically, this has led to calls for \"levelling up\" funding to regenerate northern towns.\n\nHowever, the evidence has limits: the map does not show where the healthiest high streets are, and there are pockets of poor life expectancy in the south, such as parts of London and Essex. Overall, the health of high streets reflects wider economic inequality rather than being its main cause.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::8(a)(i)": "They are all stakeholders with different interests in the coastal defences, so interviewing them gives a range of views and a more balanced picture.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::8(a)(ii)": "Environmentalists would have useful views because they could comment on the impact of the defences on beach habitats and wildlife without having a financial interest in the outcome.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::8(a)(iii)": "Researching the council's Shoreline Management Plan would have helped the students understand what was being done and why, so they could plan sensible questions to ask the planning officer.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::8(a)(iv)": "1. They have different roles: the planning officer works for the council, so has a vested interest in saying the scheme is money well spent.\n2. They have different financial interests: the café owner depends on tourists coming to the beach, so strongly supports protecting it, while the elderly resident gains nothing directly and would rather the money was spent on local services.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::9(a)(i)": "They are all local stakeholders who are affected differently by flood defences, so the students would get a range of contrasting views.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::9(a)(ii)": "Homeowners who live close to the river would have useful views because flooding threatens their homes and the value of their property.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::9(a)(iii)": "Old newspaper articles and photographs of previous floods would show how often and how badly the town had flooded, helping the students understand why the defences were built and plan their questions.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::9(a)(iv)": "1. The planning officer works for the council that paid for the defences, so has a vested interest in claiming the scheme is successful.\n2. The café owner's business depends on protecting the town centre from flooding, whereas the elderly resident thinks the money would be better spent on services and suspects politicians are protecting their own businesses.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::10(a)(i)": "C — convenient; E — friendly.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::10(a)(ii)": "The two groups had very different views. The students were mostly negative — their largest words were \"clone\", \"dump\", \"old\", \"abandoned\" and \"dead\", and the only clear positives were \"cool\" and \"fun\". The residents were much more positive, with \"convenient\" and \"friendly\" the most common words, followed by \"cheery\" and \"tidy\", although they also used some negative words such as \"deprived\", \"grim\" and \"threatening\". There is little overlap, but \"deprived\", \"dark\", \"threatening\" and \"old\" appear in both.\n\nOne reason is knowledge: the students were giving first impressions of an unfamiliar place, so they judged it on its appearance — empty shops and litter — whereas residents know the town well and value its services and community. Residents may also be biased in favour of their own town. Their purpose for being there matters too: \"convenient\" suggests residents were there to shop.\n\nThe method also explains some variation. Only 20 residents were asked, on one Saturday morning in June, so the sample may not be representative of all ages or of people who work in the week, and some may even have been visitors. Some words are ambiguous (\"changing\", \"old\"), and some students may not have taken the task seriously (\"fun\", \"unbelievable\").\n\nOverall, the main reason for the variation is the difference between outsiders' first impressions and residents' everyday experience, but the small sample and timing mean the results should be treated with caution.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::11(a)(i)": "C — tidy; E — friendly.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p2::11(a)(ii)": "The students' views were mostly negative or neutral — \"boring\", \"quiet\", \"dead\", \"empty\" — although some described the village as \"pretty\" and \"picturesque\". The residents were much more positive: \"friendly\", \"tidy\" and \"community\" were the most common words, but they also mentioned problems such as \"expensive\", \"noisy\", \"holidaymakers\" and \"incomers\".\n\nThe main reason is that students saw the village briefly as outsiders, judging only its appearance and lack of activity, whereas residents value the community spirit they have experienced over many years. Residents' concerns about expense, incomers and holidaymakers show issues outsiders would not notice, such as second homes pushing up house prices and tourists crowding the village in summer.\n\nThe method also affects the results: a random sample of only 20 residents on one Saturday morning in June might include visitors or miss certain age groups; some words are ambiguous (\"quiet\" could be positive or negative); and some students may not have taken it seriously (\"rubbish\"). Overall, the differences reflect outsiders' and insiders' contrasting experiences of the village, but the small, one-off sample limits how far we can trust the results.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::1(a)": "2002.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::1(b)": "As people become wealthier, they buy more goods such as cars and electronics and eat more meat, so each person uses more resources.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::1(c)": "Ghana's population already doubled in the last 30 years, from 15 million in 1990 to 30 million in 2020, so if this trend continues it could double again to about 60 million by 2050.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::1(d)": "1. Forest has been cleared to make space for the mine and its machinery.\n2. Polluted water from the mining kills plants and trees around the site.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::1(e)": "Without vegetation, there are no tree roots to bind the soil together, so heavy tropical rain washes the topsoil away, causing soil erosion.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::2(a)(i)": "C — between August and September.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::2(a)(ii)": "80 mm (January and December).",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::2(b)": "A — drip-tip leaves; E — buttress roots.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::2(c)": "1. The tall emergent and canopy trees have been removed, so birds, monkeys and insects that lived high in the trees lose their habitat and must migrate or die.\n2. The plantation has only one main type of tree instead of a wide variety of fruiting and flowering plants, so many animals lose their food sources and the number of species falls.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::3(a)(i)": "Gold.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::3(a)(ii)": "C — (5194 − 3100) ÷ 3100 × 100.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::3(b)": "Angola (1956).",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::3(c)": "1. Oil is currently only about a quarter of Ghana's exports (Figure 5), so developing new oil fields would increase export earnings and the share of oil in the economy, helping to replace the US$1,256 million of aid Ghana still receives.\n2. Ghana's GDP per capita (US$5,194) is lower than all three of Africa's top oil producers (US$6,100–6,800, Figure 6), so new oil could raise GDP per capita. However, Figure 6 shows that 37–52% of people in those countries live in extreme poverty, so the wealth might not reach ordinary Ghanaians.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::3(d)": "B — 100 km.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::3(e)": "Physical reason: oil only forms in certain sedimentary rocks from organic remains buried over millions of years, so it is only found where these geological conditions exist.\nEconomic reason: extracting oil, especially offshore, needs very expensive specialist drilling equipment and technology, so poorer countries can only develop their oil if a foreign TNC is willing to invest.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::3(f)": "The two views share some common ground. Both writers agree that TNCs play a central role in Ghana's resource industries: the minister admits Ghana relies on foreign TNCs for skilled workers and technology, and the professor says TNCs keep most of the profits. Both see dependence on foreign aid and a lack of skills as problems that need solving, and both recognise that mistakes have been made with natural resources.\n\nHowever, their views on the future are very different. The minister is optimistic: he believes Ghana can learn from other countries' mistakes, that new Kosmos survey data shows Ghana will become \"very rich and successful\", and that production of about 100 million barrels a year will free Ghana from aid. He sees TNCs as temporary partners until Ghana's universities train its own engineers. The professor is pessimistic: he argues that only 2% of gold profits return to Ghana, that oil is \"not much better\", that TNCs take US$100 billion a year from developing countries, and that this is \"a new version of colonialism\" which keeps Ghana too poor to build its own infrastructure and skills.\n\nOverall, the views differ much more than they agree. The main difference is their attitude to TNCs — partners for development or exploiters — which reflects their positions: the minister represents the government's oil strategy, while the professor takes a more critical, dependency-theory view.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::3(g)": "1. Figure 9 shows coal emissions rose sharply from about 8.5 billion tonnes in 2000 to about 15 billion tonnes by 2013. This is because emerging economies such as China and India industrialised rapidly, building coal-fired power stations to supply factories and growing cities.\n2. Total emissions rose from about 24.5 to 36 billion tonnes, with oil and gas also increasing. Rising populations and growing affluence mean more people own cars, use air-conditioning and fly, increasing demand for oil and gas.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::3(h)": "Figure 10 shows that climate change could bring several serious environmental challenges to Ghana. A temperature rise of 1.4–5.8 °C by 2080 and 4% less rainfall by 2040 could make the climate too dry for rainforest, which may be replaced by grassland with fewer habitats, causing irreversible loss of species. Less rainfall would also reduce freshwater supplies and the water available for hydroelectric power from the Volta dam.\n\nRising sea levels of 75–190 mm by 2100 threaten coastal urban areas where 2 million people live, as well as farmland near Lake Volta. Warmer coastal waters could cause cold-water species to disappear and food webs to collapse, threatening the livelihoods of 2 million people who fish.\n\nThe severity of these challenges depends on how much temperatures rise: the projected range is very wide, and the figure warns that the impacts will be worse the longer \"business as usual\" fossil fuel use continues. The combination of impacts is especially threatening for farmers and fishing communities. Overall, I think the loss of ecosystems and the collapse of marine food webs are the most serious challenges, because they are hard to reverse and many people depend on them for food and income.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2022-p3::4": "I think Option 2 — continuing to support the gold, cocoa and oil industries — is the best option for Ghana's future development, provided it is combined with reforms.\n\nOption 2 keeps Ghana's economy diverse. Figure 5 shows that gold, oil and cocoa together make up most of Ghana's US$21 billion of exports, and nearly half of Ghana's people work in agriculture, including cocoa, while about 1 million work in small gold mines. Supporting all three spreads the risk: if the oil price falls, as it often does, gold and cocoa can still earn income. This has already helped GDP per capita rise from US$3,100 to US$5,194 between 2010 and 2019 and reduced aid from US$1,697 million to US$1,256 million, so Ghana is moving \"beyond aid\". The income could fund education: university attendance has already risen from 7% to 17%.\n\nHowever, Option 2 has drawbacks. Cocoa and gold mining are major causes of deforestation (Figures 2 and 4), and Ghana lost a larger share of its rainforest than any other country in 2018. The professor in Figure 8 also warns that only 2% of gold profits stay in Ghana. These problems could be reduced by stricter regulation of mining, afforestation, Fairtrade schemes that pay cocoa farmers more, and requiring TNCs to process resources in Ghana so more profit stays in the country.\n\nI rejected Option 3 because relying mainly on oil is risky. Figure 6 shows that in Africa's top oil producers, 37–52% of people still live in extreme poverty, so oil wealth does not necessarily trickle down. Stopping new gold mining and cocoa production would also put millions of people out of work. Oil also adds to climate change, which Figure 10 shows threatens Ghana's forests, coastline and fisheries.\n\nI rejected Option 1 because, although education is vital for long-term sustainable development, stopping oil exploration would remove an important source of income in the short term, and Ghana's population is expected to nearly double by 2050, so it needs income now to pay for schools.\n\nOverall, Option 2 gives the best balance of economic, social and environmental goals: it provides jobs and income now, which can be invested in education and greener industries, while the environmental damage can be reduced through better management.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::1(a)(i)": "B — 60°N and 0°.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::1(a)(ii)": "X = Polar cell (A); Y = Hadley cell (E).",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::1(b)": "Ice cores from Antarctica contain bubbles of trapped air, which can be analysed to find the concentration of carbon dioxide at the time the ice formed. Higher concentrations are associated with warmer inter-glacial periods, so the cores show how the climate has changed over hundreds of thousands of years.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::1(c)": "A composite (strato) volcano.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::1(d)": "A — Conservative boundaries.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::1(e)": "Some earthquakes have a shallow focus, so the energy released has only a short distance to travel to the surface. This means the ground shaking is more intense, causing large numbers of buildings to collapse and people to be killed or injured.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::1(f)(i)": "B — Bangkok.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::1(f)(ii)": "A decline of 87% leaves 13%: 117 × 0.13 = 15.21 billion US$.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::1(g)": "1. Tropical cyclones need warm ocean water of at least 26.5–27 °C, which is found in the tropics. The warm water heats the air above it so that warm, moist air rises rapidly, providing the energy for the storm.\n2. They need the Coriolis force to make the storm spin. The Coriolis force is too weak within 5° of the equator, so cyclones cannot start rotating there; they therefore form between 5° and 30°.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::1(h)": "1. Poorer countries often lack the money to build storm-surge defences and strong buildings, so homes made of weak materials are easily destroyed by high winds and flooding.\n2. Some countries lack satellite technology and warning systems, so people are not alerted in time to evacuate, which leads to more deaths.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::1(i)": "The climate has always changed naturally. Variations in solar output linked to sunspot activity have coincided with warmer and cooler periods, such as the Little Ice Age. Large volcanic eruptions eject ash and sulphur dioxide that reflect solar radiation and cool the Earth for a year or two. Over tens of thousands of years, Milankovitch cycles — changes in the Earth's orbit (eccentricity), tilt and wobble — alter the amount of solar energy reaching the Earth and have caused the cycle of glacials and inter-glacials. Asteroid collisions can also block sunlight.\n\nHowever, since the Industrial Revolution human activities have released huge amounts of greenhouse gases. Burning fossil fuels for energy, industry and transport releases carbon dioxide; farming livestock and rice releases methane; and some gases, such as CFCs, are only produced by humans. These gases trap heat in the atmosphere, enhancing the natural greenhouse effect. Atmospheric carbon dioxide has risen from about 280 ppm to over 410 ppm, faster than at any time in the ice-core record, and global temperatures have risen by over 1 °C.\n\nNatural causes cannot explain this recent warming: solar output has not increased significantly in recent decades, and Milankovitch cycles work over thousands of years. Volcanic eruptions only have short-term effects. Therefore, while natural causes were the most important drivers of climate change over the Earth's long history, I agree that in the last 150 years human activities have become far more important, because they explain the speed and scale of current warming.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::2(a)(i)": "D — Malaysia.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::2(a)(ii)": "(2608 + 1547 + 2270 + 3526 + 10412) ÷ 5 = 20363 ÷ 5 = 4072.6 US$.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::2(a)(iii)": "A comparative bar chart, with the five countries along the x-axis and literacy rate (%) on the y-axis, with two bars for each country — one for 2011 and one for 2020.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::2(a)(iv)": "Governments have invested more money in education, building more schools and training teachers, so more children can attend school and learn to read.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::2(b)": "NGO-led projects usually involve consulting local people, so they meet the real needs of the community. They often use appropriate technology, such as hand pumps, which local people can operate and repair themselves, so the project continues to work long after the NGO leaves.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::2(c)(i)": "C — 38%.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::2(c)(ii)": "48% − 34% = 14%.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::2(d)": "1. Farming becomes mechanised as countries develop, so machines such as tractors replace workers and fewer people are needed on farms.\n2. Industrialisation creates jobs in factories and services in cities that are better paid than farming, so people migrate from rural to urban areas to take these jobs.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::2(e)": "As a country industrialises, it builds more factories and power stations that burn fossil fuels such as coal, which increases its greenhouse gas emissions.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::2(f)": "Named emerging country: India.\n1. India has become a much more important trading partner for other countries, because it has a large, skilled, English-speaking workforce, which has attracted TNCs to outsource IT and call centre work there.\n2. India has a growing political role: it is a member of the G20, which works to stabilise the global economy, and it is now one of the largest contributors of troops to UN peacekeeping missions.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::2(g)": "Named emerging country: India.\n\nIndia's location has been important to its development. It has a long coastline in the centre of the Indian Ocean, with the west coast giving easy sea links to the Middle East, Africa and Europe, and the east coast linking it to the fast-growing economies of South-East Asia. This makes it a transit point for shipping and has helped it trade. Coastal cities such as Mumbai have grown into major ports and business centres that attract TNCs.\n\nHowever, location also has disadvantages. The Himalayas form a barrier to land-based trade with China, and conflict with Pakistan over Kashmir has hampered trade with its neighbour. Development has also been uneven: landlocked states such as Bihar have attracted little foreign investment because they have no direct access to the coast to export goods.\n\nOther factors have arguably been more important. Economic liberalisation in 1991 opened India to foreign direct investment and reduced barriers to trade, leading to rapid growth. India has a large, young, educated and English-speaking workforce, which has attracted IT and service TNCs such as call centres in Bangalore. The government has also relaxed corporate taxes and improved infrastructure.\n\nOverall, India's location provided the opportunity for trade, but it was government policy from 1991 and its educated workforce that allowed it to take advantage of this. Location is therefore an important but not the most important factor, as shown by the fact that India's location did not lead to rapid development before liberalisation.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::3(a)(i)": "B — The movement of people towards the edge of a city.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::3(a)(ii)": "Increased car ownership and better roads mean people can live in rural areas and commute to work in the city, so they move out to enjoy a quieter environment with less pollution.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::3(b)(i)": "Plot a point where GNI per capita US$3,600 on the x-axis meets 24% on the y-axis.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::3(b)(ii)": "Draw a straight line rising from the bottom left to the top right, following the general trend so that there is roughly an equal number of points above and below it.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::3(b)(iii)": "Highest 70% (Botswana) − lowest 20% (Malawi) = 50%.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::3(b)(iv)": "There is a positive relationship: as GNI per capita increases, the percentage of the population living in urban areas increases. For example, Malawi has a GNI of about US$600 and only 20% urban, whereas Botswana has a GNI over US$6,600 and 70% urban. Angola is an anomaly, as it has 67% urban population but a GNI of only about US$2,200.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::3(c)": "1. Land values: land in the CBD is the most expensive, so only shops and offices that can afford high rents locate there, and buildings are tall to make the most of the space.\n2. Accessibility: the edge of the city is near ring roads and motorways, so manufacturing industry and out-of-town retail parks locate there, as they need to move goods and attract customers by car.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::3(d)(i)": "12.1 : 36.3 = 1 : 3.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::3(d)(ii)": "The number of megacities is projected to increase from 13 in 2000 to 34 in 2025 — more than double. Most of the increase is in Asia, with India having the most megacities by 2025, whereas in 2000 Japan and the USA had the most. South America increases from 1 to 4, while North America has the same number.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::3(d)(iii)": "1. Rural–urban migration: many people in Asia, where the number of megacities rises sharply by 2025, move to cities for better-paid jobs in factories and services.\n2. Natural increase: migrants to cities are mostly young adults, so birth rates are higher than death rates; this explains the growth of new megacities in Africa, such as Lagos.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p1::3(e)": "Named megacity: Mumbai.\n\nSustainability means improving people's lives socially, economically and environmentally without harming future generations.\n\nVision Mumbai is a top-down strategy led by the government, which aimed to improve the city's infrastructure and remove slums such as Dharavi. It has rehoused thousands of slum dwellers in new apartment blocks with water and sanitation, which has improved health. Because it is government-funded, it can work at a city-wide scale. However, many residents are unhappy: the new flats are often poor quality, rents are too high for former slum dwellers, and communities and informal businesses — such as the recycling industries in Dharavi — have been broken up. This means it is not socially or economically sustainable for many of the poorest people.\n\nBottom-up strategies are led by NGOs working with communities. SPARC works with local people to build community toilet blocks, which residents help to build and pay a small monthly fee to use. This improves sanitation and health, is affordable, and gives communities ownership, so the blocks are maintained. The Hamara Foundation provides education for street children, improving their future job prospects. These projects are low-cost and meet local needs, but they only help relatively small numbers of people compared with Mumbai's population of over 20 million.\n\nOverall, bottom-up strategies have been more successful in achieving sustainability because they meet local needs and are affordable and maintained by the community. However, they cannot solve city-wide problems such as transport and water supply, which need top-down investment. The most sustainable approach is a combination, in which top-down projects involve local communities in decisions.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::1(a)": "A — South-east England.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::1(b)": "Freeze-thaw weathering: water enters cracks and joints in the rock and freezes at night, expanding by about 9%. This puts pressure on the rock, and repeated freezing and thawing eventually breaks pieces off.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::1(c)": "Valley glaciers eroded the landscape by plucking and abrasion, turning V-shaped river valleys into wide, steep-sided U-shaped valleys, such as those in the Lake District.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::2(a)": "Waves force water and air into the joints and faults, compressing the air; when the wave retreats the pressure is released, which widens the cracks (hydraulic action) until blocks of rock break off.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::2(b)": "Soft engineering such as beach nourishment is relatively cheap compared with hard engineering such as sea walls, so a larger stretch of coast can be protected for the same cost.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::2(c)": "Named coastal landscape: the Holderness coast.\n1. Coastal defences such as the groynes and rock armour at Mappleton trap sediment, which starves beaches further south of material, so erosion has increased there.\n2. Tourism and caravan parks along the coast increase trampling of cliff tops and dunes, removing vegetation and making the land more easily eroded.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::3(a)(i)": "B — Some trees are partly under water.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::3(a)(ii)": "The water is brown and muddy.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::3(b)": "Cost: hard engineering such as flood walls is very expensive to build and to maintain, which can take money away from other services.\nBenefit: it protects homes and businesses from flooding, saving large sums of money in damage and protecting people's lives.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::4": "Figure 2a shows that major flood events have become far more frequent: there were only 1–3 per twenty-year period between 1920 and 1999, but 17 in 2000–2019, which is 17 of the 24 events in the whole period. Figure 2b shows that three of the five largest events (2007, 2013 and 2016) occurred since 2007. However, deaths have fallen: 307 people died in 1953 and 14 in 1928, compared with 13 in 2007 and none in 2013 and 2016.\n\nPhysical reasons may explain the increase. Climate change is likely to be increasing the frequency and intensity of storms and heavy rainfall, which increases river discharge — the 2007 and 2016 floods were caused by heavy rainfall and Storm Desmond. Rising sea levels and the sinking of south-east England make coastal flooding from storm surges more likely, as in 1953 and 2013.\n\nHuman reasons are also important. The population has grown, especially in London and south-east England, so more houses have been built on floodplains, and urbanisation creates impermeable surfaces that increase surface run-off; this explains why 55,000 properties flooded in 2007. On the other hand, the fall in deaths can be explained by human improvements: better forecasting and warning systems from the Met Office and Environment Agency, and defences such as the Thames Barrier, built after 1953.\n\nOverall, physical changes linked to climate change have probably increased the number of flood events, but human factors explain both the large number of properties affected and the fall in deaths. Because events are recorded better today, some of the increase may also reflect improved record keeping.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::5(a)": "London is much more ethnically diverse than the North East. White British is the largest group in both, but it is only 36.8% in London compared with 90.6% in the North East. All other groups are much larger in London: for example, Asian people make up 20.7% in London but only 3.7% in the North East, and White Other is 17.0% compared with 2.4%.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::5(b)": "1. Large cities such as London have more jobs, so international migrants tend to move there to find work.\n2. Migrants often settle where there are already established communities from the same ethnic group, with familiar places of worship, shops and support networks, so diversity becomes concentrated in particular cities.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::6(a)(i)": "B — 2009.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::6(a)(ii)": "Bath's population growth fluctuated much more than that of England and Wales, which was more consistent.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::6(b)": "9,000 ÷ 211,000 × 100 = 4.26 = 4.3%.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::6(c)": "UK city: London.\n1. The Congestion Charge and ULEZ charge drivers to enter central London, which has reduced traffic and so reduced air pollution and carbon emissions.\n2. Santander Cycles bike hire and cycle superhighways encourage people to cycle instead of drive, reducing fossil fuel use and improving health.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::6(d)": "UK city: London. The Docklands has grown because it was regenerated by the London Docklands Development Corporation. Derelict docks were redeveloped into offices such as Canary Wharf, which attracted investment from banks and financial firms. This created thousands of jobs, so people moved into new housing in the area, and transport links such as the DLR and Elizabeth line made it even more accessible, encouraging further growth.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::7": "Figure 6 shows that England's rural population is much older than its urban population. The rural modal age group is 55–59 (8.0%), whereas the urban modal group is 30–34 (7.2%). Urban areas have a higher percentage in every age group from 0–4 up to 40–44, while rural areas have a higher percentage in every group from 45–49 upwards — for example, 70–74 is 7.1% rural compared with 4.5% urban.\n\nOne cause is the out-migration of young people from rural areas. Rural areas have fewer jobs and a narrower range of jobs, and few universities, so people aged 18–30 move to cities for education and work; this explains the low rural percentages for 20–34 (for example 4.2% at 20–24 compared with 6.5% in urban areas). Rural housing is often expensive, particularly in attractive areas, so it is hard for young local people to stay.\n\nA second cause is in-migration of older people. Many people retire to the countryside for a quieter life, and others move through counter-urbanisation once they are established in their careers, selling urban homes to buy larger rural ones; most rural people live within an hour of a large town, so they can commute. This explains the bulge from 45 to 74.\n\nCities, by contrast, attract young international migrants and young adults at the family-forming stage, which also explains the slightly higher urban percentages of children aged 0–4 (5.9% compared with 4.5%), suggesting a lower birth rate in rural areas.\n\nIt is also important to remember that the urban population (47 million) is nearly five times larger than the rural population (10 million), so even small percentages in urban areas represent many more people. Overall, migration — young people leaving and older people arriving — is the main cause of the differences, rather than differences in birth and death rates.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::8(a)(i)": "The beach was only a 10-minute walk from school, so little time was lost travelling and they could return easily in December.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::8(a)(ii)": "They wanted to see whether the beach profile changed between seasons, because winter storms bring more destructive waves that could change the gradient of the beach.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::8(a)(iii)": "The beach was steeper in December than in May, especially at Profile 1: for example, site 2 was 6° in May but 12° in December.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::8(a)(iv)": "1. An OS map would help them plan the location of the two profiles so they could find exactly the same places again when they returned in December.\n2. Records from the council responsible for coastal management would tell them when beach replenishment had taken place, which could explain differences in the profiles.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::8(b)": "Named fieldwork location: Swanage Bay, Dorset.\n\nWe collected quantitative data on beach profiles using ranging poles, a tape measure and a clinometer at 5 m intervals along transects, and measured sediment size with a ruler at each point, at sites on either side of groynes.\n\nOne strength of the beach-profile method was that it was simple to carry out with uncomplicated equipment, so every group could collect data at several sites, and the results could be compared directly between the up-drift and down-drift sides of the groynes to show the effect of management on longshore drift. The beach was also safe and publicly accessible at low tide.\n\nHowever, there were weaknesses. Reading the clinometer was open to human error, especially as the poles were sometimes not held vertically on soft sand, so gradient values may have been inaccurate. Sediment measurements were based on only ten pebbles per site, chosen by hand, which may have introduced bias; random sampling with a quadrat would have been more reliable. We only visited on one day, so the results reflect conditions on that day and may not be typical of how management affects processes over a year.\n\nOverall, the methods were appropriate for our enquiry because they produced comparable data that showed the build-up of sediment on the up-drift side of groynes, but their accuracy and reliability were limited by human error, small samples and a single visit. Repeating measurements and visiting in different seasons would improve reliability.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::9(a)(i)": "The river was only a 10-minute walk from school, so little time was lost travelling and it was easy to return in December.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::9(a)(ii)": "They expected discharge to be different in winter, because higher rainfall and lower evaporation in December would increase the amount of water in the river.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::9(a)(iii)": "Discharge increased downstream: in May it was only 0.05 cumecs at site 1 in Section 1 but 0.35 cumecs at site 6 in Section 2, because tributaries and surface run-off add water downstream.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::9(a)(iv)": "1. An OS map would help them choose and record sites so they could return to the same places months later.\n2. Environment Agency river level data would let them check whether the river was at a typical level on their visit days and compare their results.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::9(b)": "Named fieldwork location: the River Holford, Somerset.\n\nWe collected quantitative data on channel width with a tape measure, depth with a metre rule at regular intervals across the channel, and velocity using a float timed over 10 m, at five sites downstream, so we could calculate discharge and assess flood risk.\n\nA strength was that the methods were simple and cheap, so each group could measure several sites, giving a good range of data to compare. Measuring depth at regular intervals gave a fair average cross-section.\n\nHowever, the float method was inaccurate: floats were caught by eddies and vegetation and timing by stopwatch involved reaction errors, so we repeated each measurement three times and took the average, which improved reliability. Some sites were difficult to access safely, so we chose sites where the banks were low, which may not have been representative. We only visited on one day in normal flow, so our data does not show the river in flood, which limits what it tells us about flood risk.\n\nOverall, the methods allowed us to identify the increase in discharge downstream, but accuracy was limited by the float method and reliability by the single visit. A flow meter and repeat visits after heavy rain would make the data more useful for assessing flood risk.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::10(a)": "Named urban location: Bristol. We had been studying deprivation in urban areas, so we investigated whether environmental quality improves with distance from the city centre, as urban models predict.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::10(b)": "1. We completed an environmental quality survey at ten sites along a transect, scoring factors such as litter, noise and building condition from −2 to +2, which gave quantitative data.\n2. We asked residents a questionnaire about their views of quality of life in their area and recorded key points as qualitative data.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::10(c)": "Our hypothesis was that environmental quality improves with distance from the city centre. It was mostly supported, as scores rose from −8 near the centre to +12 in the suburbs, but not consistently: one site near a busy main road had a low score because of traffic noise, showing local factors also matter.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::10(d)": "The first conclusion is mostly supported for crime: about 61% of older people and 69% of younger people disagree that crime is a major problem. However, it is not supported for noise, where 54% of older people and 60% of younger people agree that noise and anti-social behaviour are major problems, and 60% of older people do not feel safe alone in the evening. So the majority are not happy on every measure, and the statements cover only three aspects of quality of life.\n\nThe second conclusion is better supported: older people are more worried about safety (60% agree they feel unsafe, compared with 35% of younger people) and slightly more concerned about crime (39% compared with 31%). However, on noise, younger people agree slightly more (60% compared with 54%), so the conclusion is not true for every statement.\n\nThe method affects reliability. There were more younger (42) than older (28) respondents, so combining them would bias the overall result. Selecting people of their \"own age\" and \"older\" was subjective, and there was no check that respondents lived in the town rather than visiting. Surveying only on one Saturday morning may not give a representative cross-section, and words such as \"anti-social behaviour\" mean different things to different people. However, 70 questionnaires is a reasonable sample, and the contrasts between age groups are clear. Overall, conclusion 2 is partly reliable, but conclusion 1 is unreliable.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::11(a)": "Named rural location: Castleton, Derbyshire. We had been studying rural change and deprivation, so we investigated whether environmental quality and residents' views differ between the village centre and its edge.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::11(b)": "1. We completed an environmental quality survey at ten sites across the village, scoring factors such as litter, traffic noise and building condition, giving quantitative data.\n2. We asked villagers a questionnaire about their views of quality of life and recorded key points as qualitative data.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::11(c)": "Our hypothesis was that environmental quality is lower in the village centre because of tourist traffic. It was partly supported: centre scores were lower, mainly because of traffic and parking, but one edge site near a quarry road also scored poorly, so the pattern was not consistent.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p2::11(d)": "The first conclusion is mostly supported for crime: about 61% of older people and 69% of younger people disagree that crime is a major problem. However, it is not supported for noise, where 54% of older people and 60% of younger people agree that noise and anti-social behaviour are major problems, and 60% of older people do not feel safe alone in the evening. So the majority are not happy on every measure, and three statements cover only part of quality of life.\n\nThe second conclusion is better supported: older people are more worried about safety (60% agree compared with 35% of younger people) and slightly more concerned about crime (39% compared with 31%). However, younger people agree slightly more about noise (60% compared with 54%).\n\nThe method affects reliability. There were more younger (42) than older (28) respondents; selection by age was subjective; there was no check that respondents lived in the village rather than visiting; and one Saturday morning may not be representative. However, 70 questionnaires is a reasonable sample and the contrasts are clear. Overall, conclusion 2 is partly reliable, but conclusion 1 is unreliable.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::1(a)(i)": "Tundra.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::1(a)(ii)": "C and E.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::1(b)": "Tropical rainforest.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::1(c)": "1. Large glaciers and ice caps cover the high central areas of Iceland, where cold temperatures and the lack of soil prevent vegetation growing, so there is no forest there.\n2. The coastal edges, especially in the south-west, have a milder maritime climate and lower altitude, so forest could grow there, which is why taiga was found near the coast.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::2(a)(i)": "D — The number of plant and animal species.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::2(a)(ii)": "Human reason: the Vikings and their descendants cut down the forest for timber, fuel and to clear land for sheep; the sheep then grazed on young saplings, stopping the forest regenerating.\nPhysical reason: once the trees were removed, strong winds eroded the soil, so there was no longer enough soil or nutrients for new trees to grow.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::2(b)": "Replanting will create jobs in forestry and timber production, which boosts incomes and creates a multiplier effect in rural areas.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::2(c)": "Taiga trees have thin, needle-shaped leaves with a waxy coating, which reduces water loss through transpiration when the ground is frozen and water cannot be taken up.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::3(a)(i)": "Tourism (27,000 people).",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::3(a)(ii)": "Fishing and food.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::3(b)(i)": "D — 13.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::3(b)(ii)": "A war in an oil-producing region, such as the Russian invasion of Ukraine.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::3(c)": "1. Iceland's fishing fleet (27% of earnings) and tourism, where almost all tourists arrive by air, depend on oil, because ships and aeroplanes cannot yet run on geothermal energy or HEP — so oil still makes up 16% of the energy mix.\n2. Relying on imported oil is risky: in the 1970s Iceland could not afford oil after a global price rise, so having cheap renewable energy for heating 90% of homes and powering industry protects people from price shocks.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::3(d)": "C — 53 – 15.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::3(e)": "Physical reason: some countries, such as the UK, are far from plate boundaries, so they cannot use geothermal energy, and they may have few large rivers suitable for HEP.\nPolitical reason: governments that earn money from oil and gas sales, such as Saudi Arabia, have little incentive to subsidise renewable energy.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::3(f)": "Renewable energy in Iceland has had significant environmental impacts. The Kárahnjúkar HEP station flooded a large area of land to create its reservoir; this destroyed the breeding grounds of wild reindeer and geese and caused some river fish species to die out, so it has reduced biodiversity. The Hellisheidi geothermal plant brings sulphur and nitrogen gases to the surface with the hot water, causing soil and air pollution, and health problems in Reykjavik may be linked to it.\n\nThere are also indirect impacts. Cheap renewable electricity has attracted three aluminium TNCs, which use three-quarters of Iceland's electricity; bauxite is shipped to Iceland, and processing it causes pollution and emissions, so renewable energy has encouraged heavy industry.\n\nHowever, renewable energy also has major benefits: geothermal energy heats 90% of homes and, together with HEP, means Iceland uses far less fossil fuel for electricity, so it produces very low carbon emissions compared with coal or oil.\n\nOverall, I think the HEP scheme has had the worst impact because it permanently destroyed a large area of habitat, whereas geothermal pollution is more localised. The impacts are significant locally, but at a global scale Iceland's renewable energy is far less damaging than fossil fuels would be.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::3(g)(i)": "2014.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::3(g)(ii)": "Rising ocean water temperatures cause habitat loss for cold-water species such as cod.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::3(h)": "Some parts of Iceland's plan would be very valuable for other countries. Encouraging citizens to conserve energy depends only on behaviour, so any country could adopt it. Banning new petrol and diesel vehicles after 2030 and cutting road emissions by 20% is similar to the UK's own plans, and reducing industrial emissions by 43% in line with EU targets is a goal shared by many European countries. Replanting forests to increase carbon storage is a low-technology approach that many countries could use to offset emissions.\n\nHowever, other parts are less transferable. Iceland can promote renewable energy so strongly because it has unusual physical geography — geothermal energy from its position on a plate boundary and glacial meltwater for HEP; most countries do not have this potential. Carbon capture and storage technology is expensive and unproven, so poorer countries may not be able to afford it. Afforestation needs large areas of available land and a suitable climate, which densely populated or arid countries may not have.\n\nOverall, the plan has value as a model, particularly its focus on transport, energy conservation and tree planting, but countries would need to adapt it to their own resources and level of development. The most useful part for all countries is probably citizen energy conservation, as it works in any context.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2023-p3::4": "Chosen option: Option 2 — increased planting of new trees to replace Iceland's lost taiga forest.\n\nI think Iceland's government should focus on replanting trees first because it offers the best balance of environmental, economic and social benefits. Environmentally, replanting would reverse the damage caused since the Vikings cleared the forest, when cover fell from 25% to less than 1% by 1900 (Figures 1 and 3). New forest would protect the soil from wind erosion, increase biodiversity by restoring habitats, reduce flood risk, and store carbon, helping meet the Climate Action Plan goal of increasing carbon storage by 500% (Figure 10). Economically, it creates forestry jobs and timber sales, and socially it provides recreation (Figure 4). Unlike the other options, it does not threaten existing jobs.\n\nThere are drawbacks. So much soil has been eroded that trees may struggle to grow (Figure 3), and planting 3 million fast-growing pine trees each year may change the landscape that tourists want to see (Figure 4), which matters because tourism provides 33% of earnings and 27,000 jobs (Figure 5). Non-native pines may also not support the same biodiversity as the original birch woodland. However, these risks could be reduced by planting native species and protecting key scenic areas.\n\nOption 1 would bring immediate benefits where pollution is worst, but aluminium is Iceland's most valuable export and TNCs are footloose: they are attracted by low taxes and energy prices and might relocate if costs rise (Figures 5 and 8), threatening 4,000 jobs. Damage at Kárahnjúkar has already been done, so new laws would not reverse it.\n\nOption 3 is important for climate change, because transport is the main remaining use of fossil fuels (16% oil in 2018 — Figure 6). However, fishing boats and aeroplanes cannot yet run on renewable energy, so cutting fossil fuels quickly could harm fishing (27% of earnings) and tourism, which depend on them. Electric cars are already planned from 2030, so this is partly underway.\n\nOverall, Option 2 should come first because it has long-term benefits for the environment and climate without damaging the economy, and Iceland's tiny population means its global emission cuts would have little effect, so an option with clear local benefits is most sustainable.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(a)(i)": "Draw a bar for April rising from 0 to 300 mm on the rainfall (right-hand) axis, the same width as the other bars.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(a)(ii)": "D — November.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(a)(iii)": "26.3 − 25.1 = 1.2 °C.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(b)": "Because of the curvature of the Earth, the sun's rays hit the equator directly, so their energy is concentrated on a small area; at the poles the rays hit at a low angle and are spread over a much larger area, so it is colder.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(c)": "Large eruptions inject ash and sulphur dioxide high into the atmosphere, which reflect incoming solar radiation, so global temperatures fall for a year or two.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(d)(i)": "157,000 ÷ 5 = 31,400.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(d)(ii)": "At a convergent boundary, the denser oceanic plate subducts beneath the other plate. Friction causes the plates to stick and pressure builds up until they suddenly slip, releasing energy as an earthquake.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(e)": "Chosen hazard: earthquake. Buildings can be designed to be aseismic, for example with rubber shock absorbers in their foundations and cross-bracing, so they sway rather than collapse during shaking, meaning fewer people are trapped and killed in rubble.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(f)(i)": "B — August and September.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(f)(ii)": "111 ÷ 294 × 100 = 37.75 = 37.8%.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(f)(iii)": "Hurricanes need ocean temperatures above 26.5 °C. Water heats slowly, so oceans only become warm enough by late summer and autumn, which is why most hurricanes occur in August and September and none from December to May.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(g)": "1. Poorer countries cannot afford to build sea walls and storm-surge barriers, or strong buildings, so more homes are destroyed and more people die.\n2. Countries with low-lying coastlines, such as Bangladesh, are more vulnerable because storm surges can flood large areas inland, especially where the funnel shape of the Bay of Bengal raises water levels.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::1(h)": "There is strong agreement among climate scientists that global temperatures will continue to rise because of greenhouse gases from human activity. Evidence from ice cores and tree rings shows how the climate has responded to past changes in carbon dioxide, and complex computer models all predict warming by 2100. In that sense the general direction of change can be predicted.\n\nHowever, it is difficult to predict the scale and consequences. Projections range from about 1.1 °C to over 5 °C by 2100, because the outcome depends on future emissions. These depend on how successfully countries implement policies such as the Paris Agreement's 1.5 °C target, how quickly they switch to renewable energy, and how fast developing and emerging countries industrialise. The growth of the middle class and consumerism in countries such as India and China could greatly increase emissions, and future population and economic growth are hard to estimate.\n\nNatural systems add further uncertainty. Positive feedback loops — for example, melting ice reducing albedo, or thawing permafrost releasing methane — could accelerate warming in ways that are hard to model. Regional consequences, such as changes in rainfall, the frequency of tropical cyclones or the speed of sea-level rise, are even harder to predict than global averages.\n\nOverall, I agree that the consequences of climate change are difficult to predict precisely, mainly because they depend on human decisions about emissions that have not yet been made. However, scientists can predict with confidence that warming will continue and that its impacts will be serious, so uncertainty is not a reason to delay action.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::2(a)": "B and E — colonialism and neo-colonialism.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::2(b)": "Gross National Income (GNI) per capita.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::2(c)": "Top-down schemes are often very expensive, so governments have to borrow money from organisations such as the World Bank, leaving the country in debt.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::2(d)(i)": "Mali: plot a point where 2 years on the x-axis meets a fertility rate of 6. Mauritius: plot a point where 10 years meets a fertility rate of 1.4.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::2(d)(ii)": "B — 2.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::2(d)(iii)": "Women who stay in education longer are more likely to have careers, so they marry and have children later in life and have fewer children overall.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::2(e)": "A and C.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::2(f)": "Frank divided the world into a developed core and an underdeveloped periphery. The periphery exports cheap raw materials to the core, which processes them into high-value manufactured goods; the profits stay in the core, so the periphery remains dependent and develops slowly, explaining why gaps in development persist over time.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::2(g)(i)": "Divide the blank 60% into three segments: China 35% (126°), USA 15% (54°) and Japan 10% (36°), drawing lines from the centre to the edge, and label each segment with its country name.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::2(g)(ii)": "Named emerging country: India.\n1. After economic liberalisation in 1991, many TNCs set up in India because of its cheap, skilled, English-speaking labour, which greatly increased exports of services such as IT and call centres, as well as manufactured goods.\n2. Rapid economic growth has changed its imports: India now imports large amounts of crude oil to fuel its industries and growing car ownership.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::2(h)": "Named emerging country: India.\n\nThere are large differences in development within India. Maharashtra, which contains Mumbai, is a core region with high incomes and economic output, while Bihar in the north-east is a periphery region where over 80% of people are subsistence farmers and literacy and school completion are much lower.\n\nLocation is one of the most important reasons. Mumbai is on the west coast with a major port linking it to Europe and the Middle East, so it attracts foreign direct investment and TNCs. Bihar is landlocked, so companies cannot easily export goods, and it attracts little investment. Once growth starts in a core region, the multiplier effect attracts more businesses and services, and rural–urban migration of young workers from places like Bihar further concentrates wealth in the cities.\n\nSocial factors also matter. School attendance is lower in rural areas, so fewer people have the skills that TNCs need. The caste system in rural areas can keep people in lower castes poor, and limited infrastructure such as roads and electricity makes rural areas less attractive for investment.\n\nHistorical factors have played a part too: during colonialism, ports such as Mumbai were developed for trade while inland areas were used to produce raw materials, setting up an unequal pattern that continues today.\n\nOverall, I think location is the most important reason, because coastal access determined where investment went after liberalisation; however, social factors such as education and caste make the gap hard to close, as they trap rural areas in a cycle of poverty.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::3(a)(i)": "D — New Delhi.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::3(a)(ii)": "Jakarta.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::3(a)(iii)": "15.23 − 11.89 = 3.34; 3.34 ÷ 11.89 × 100 = 28.09 = 28.1%.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::3(a)(iv)": "A comparative bar graph with the five cities along the x-axis and population (millions) on the y-axis, with two bars for each city — one for 2010 and one for 2025.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::3(b)": "Rural–urban migration: people move from the countryside to cities because there are more job opportunities and better services such as schools and healthcare.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::3(c)": "1. Deindustrialisation: when factories close, people lose their jobs and move away to find work elsewhere, as happened in Detroit.\n2. Counter-urbanisation: people move out to rural areas for a quieter, safer life with cheaper housing, made possible by working from home and better transport.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::3(d)(i)": "C — 65%.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::3(d)(ii)": "Bolivia's urban population increased greatly, from 45% in 1980 to 70% in 2020 (+25%), whereas Guyana's decreased slightly, from 30% to 27% (−3%). Bolivia changed most between 1980 and 2000 (+17%), while Guyana's fall was mostly between 2000 and 2020.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::3(e)(i)": "Area B has larger houses with gardens and swimming pools, whereas Area A is densely packed with small houses.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::3(e)(ii)": "1.8 : 7.2 = 1 : 4.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::3(f)": "Named megacity: Lagos.\n1. Lagos is on the coast of the Gulf of Guinea, so it developed as Nigeria's main port; about 80% of the country's imports pass through its docks, creating jobs that attract migrants.\n2. Lagos is close to Nigeria's oilfields in the Niger Delta, so oil companies and related businesses have located there, attracting thousands of people looking for work.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p1::3(g)": "Named megacity: Lagos.\n\nRapid population growth — Lagos grows by hundreds of thousands of people a year — has created serious social challenges. There is a severe housing shortage, so around two-thirds of residents live in informal settlements such as Makoko, often without electricity, sanitation or clean water. Many people have to buy water from expensive vendors, and the combination of poor sanitation and high population densities spreads water-borne diseases such as cholera and typhoid, lowering life expectancy. There are not enough formal jobs, so most people work in the informal economy with low, unreliable incomes and no protection. Residents of informal settlements have no legal rights to the land and can be forcibly evicted.\n\nThere are also major environmental challenges. Lagos produces thousands of tonnes of waste a day, much of which is not collected; at Olusosun landfill, rotting waste releases methane, which has caused fires and air pollution. Raw sewage and waste pollute Lagos Lagoon, harming fish stocks. Traffic congestion is severe, and vehicle emissions cause high levels of air pollution and respiratory problems.\n\nI think the social challenges are the most significant, because the lack of clean water and sanitation directly threatens people's health and lives every day. However, the challenges are linked: environmental problems such as water pollution and waste worsen health in the slums, so the poorest people suffer from both. Overall, the biggest challenge is the provision of basic services, as the city's infrastructure cannot keep up with its growth.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::1(a)(i)": "The Andes mountains.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::1(a)(ii)": "C — 75,000 km².",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::1(a)(iii)": "Rainforest cannot grow high in the Andes mountains because temperatures fall with altitude, so it is too cold and the growing season is too short for rainforest trees.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::1(b)(i)": "D — 2016.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::1(b)(ii)": "1. Forests are being cut down to provide resources: tropical rainforest loss rose from about 67,000 km² in 2001 to a peak of about 168,000 km² in 2016, as forest is cleared for cattle ranching, soya, logging and mining, replacing forest biomes with farmland.\n2. Climate change is altering temperature and rainfall, and because biomes are adapted to particular climates, trees may die (for example from drought or wildfires in the taiga), allowing other biomes such as grassland to expand.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::2(a)(i)": "3.5 × 2 = US$7 billion.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::2(a)(ii)": "D — The temperature is high throughout the year.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::2(b)(i)": "100 − 70.7 = 29.3% (or 17 ÷ 58 × 100 = 29.3%).",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::2(b)(ii)": "A — Preventing trade of endangered plant and animal species.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::2(c)": "Strength: organisations such as REDD allow richer countries to support poorer ones — Norway donated over US$50 million in 2022 — and most rainforest countries (70.7%) have joined, so advice and funding are shared widely.\nWeakness: funding is voluntary and far too small; Ecuador's oil is worth about US$7 billion, but not enough countries were willing to donate even half of this, so the 2007 plan collapsed.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::3(a)(i)": "Fish and seafood (US$5.1 billion).",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::3(a)(ii)": "B — 18.25.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::3(b)(i)": "Storm damage or a volcanic eruption.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::3(b)(ii)": "1. GDP per person has risen from about US$1,400 in 2000 to about US$6,000 in 2021, so people have more money to buy cars, fridges and air conditioning, which use more energy per person.\n2. The economy is growing and exports such as fish and seafood are expanding, so fishing fleets, processing factories and transport use more energy.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::3(c)": "1. HEP schemes require dams that flood large valleys, destroying habitats and forcing wildlife and people to move.\n2. Wind turbines can kill birds and bats and spoil the views of wild landscapes.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::3(d)": "Yasuní National Park faces serious threats from the oil industry. Figure 7 shows that oil production clears wide strips of rainforest and digs up the soil to lay pipelines, and Figure 8 states that 40% of Ecuador's conventional oil lies beneath the park, with companies such as Sinopec and Petroamazonas already exploring parts of it. Only some areas of the park are fully protected, so oil exploration is allowed elsewhere.\n\nThe biggest environmental threat is biodiversity loss. YNP has 750 mammal and bird species and 2,000 tree species, including jaguars, pygmy marmosets and pink river dolphins, which could be threatened by habitat loss and oil spills in rivers. This matters globally because the park's biodiversity could provide cures for diseases. Once forest is cleared, the thin tropical soils are eroded, so the damage may be permanent.\n\nThe roads and pipes built by oil companies create secondary threats, because they give access to hunters and loggers. This leads to further deforestation and to violent confrontations with the Tagaeri and Taromenane communities, whose way of life depends on the forest. These communities have not benefited from oil, so they face injustice as well as environmental damage.\n\nOverall, I think the most serious threat is the clearance of forest for roads and pipelines, because it not only destroys habitats directly but opens the forest up to further damage from loggers and hunters, threatening both wildlife and communities. The threats are unlikely to decrease while so much oil lies under the park and Ecuador needs money to pay its debts.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::3(e)": "They held a protest march in Quito and blocked roads to stop drilling equipment arriving.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::3(f)(i)": "Shale gas and tar sands.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::3(f)(ii)": "Figure 10 shows that developed countries are by far the most responsible for carbon dioxide emissions since 1750. Together they account for 58.5% — the USA alone 25%, the UK 4.5% and other developed countries 29% — even though they have only 1.2 billion people. Emerging countries account for 31% (China 15% and other emerging countries 16%), and all developing countries together for only 10.4%, despite having 4.1 billion people. Ecuador has produced just 0.1%.\n\nThis means developed countries are overwhelmingly responsible, because they industrialised first and have burned fossil fuels for over 200 years. If emissions are measured per person, the inequality is even greater: 1.2 billion people in developed countries produced more than half of all emissions, while 4.1 billion in developing countries produced a tenth.\n\nHowever, the data have limitations. They cover the whole period since 1750, so they hide recent changes: China is now the world's largest annual emitter, so its responsibility is growing quickly. The data only include industry and fossil fuels, not deforestation, which would raise the share of some developing countries. Trade also matters: many goods consumed in developed countries are made in China, so some of China's emissions are really caused by consumers elsewhere. Data from the 1700s may also be unreliable.\n\nOverall, the USA and other developed countries are most responsible historically, and developing countries such as Ecuador least responsible, which supports the argument that developing countries should be allowed to use fossil fuels for longer — although China's rapidly rising emissions mean responsibility is changing.",
  "geog-edx-1gb0-2024-p3::4": "Chosen option: Option 3 — gradually shut down Ecuador's oil industry, starting with drilling in national parks and populated rainforest areas.\n\nI think Option 3 offers the best long-term future because it protects the most valuable parts of the rainforest straight away while giving the economy time to adjust. Yasuní National Park has 750 mammal and bird species and 2,000 tree species, and oil roads give access to loggers and hunters, causing violent conflict with the Tagaeri and Taromenane communities (Figure 8). Stopping drilling there first would protect biodiversity, ecosystem services and the rights of indigenous people, who have won court cases against drilling and blocked roads to protect their land (Figure 9). In the long term, moving away from oil is also necessary to tackle climate change, and extreme weather has already harmed Ecuador's economy (Figure 6).\n\nA gradual shutdown is realistic because Ecuador has other exports it can develop: fish and seafood (US$5.1 billion) and bananas (US$3.8 billion) are already worth more than oil products (US$4.9 billion) (Figure 5). Ecotourism in the rainforest and national parks could also create jobs that depend on keeping the forest intact.\n\nThe main drawback is economic. Ecuador owes US$60 billion and its GDP growth has depended on oil prices (Introduction, Figures 5 and 6), so losing oil income could reduce spending on schools and healthcare. Some rainforest communities are willing to work with oil companies if they benefit (Figure 9).\n\nOption 1 would be ideal for the environment, but it already failed in 2007 when other countries called it blackmail and the fund collapsed by 2013 (Figure 3); REDD's donations of about US$50 million are tiny compared with the oil's US$7 billion value, so relying on other countries is too uncertain. Option 2 is arguably fair, because Ecuador has produced only 0.1% of global emissions while developed countries produced 58.5% (Figure 10), and sharing profits would help rainforest communities. However, using all the oil would destroy much of the rainforest, including 40% of the oil under YNP, and money cannot replace lost biodiversity or a way of life.\n\nOverall, Option 3 balances the environment, people and the economy: it protects the most important areas immediately, respects indigenous rights and gives Ecuador time to diversify, making it the most sustainable choice for all of Ecuador's people in the long term.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::1(a)(i)": "The southern tip of South America (southern Chile and Argentina and the ocean around it).",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::1(a)(ii)": "+0.1 to +0.2 °C per decade.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::1(a)(iii)": "The Sahara / north-east Africa (also the Middle East or northern Canada).",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::1(a)(iv)": "Add labels for the continents and major countries so that regions can be identified.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::1(b)(i)": "Plot a point at 150 above the year 2000, then draw a solid line joining it to the end of the existing line at 1999 (about 80) and to the start of the line at 2001 (about 190).",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::1(b)(ii)": "B — 11 years.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::1(c)": "Climate change is likely to bring hotter summers to the UK. More frequent heatwaves, like the 40.3 °C recorded in July 2022, increase deaths and illness among elderly and vulnerable people, putting pressure on the NHS. Warmer summers could also have positive social impacts, such as more opportunities for outdoor leisure and tourism.\n\nWetter winters and more intense storms will increase flooding. Homes may be flooded or destroyed, leaving families temporarily homeless, causing stress and mental health problems, and some homes may become impossible to insure. Rising sea levels will threaten coastal communities, and some, such as villages on the Holderness coast, may have to be abandoned.\n\nDroughts and extreme weather could make crops fail in the UK and abroad, so food prices rise. Higher prices stretch family budgets, increasing the use of food banks, especially for low-income households. Climate change may also increase the number of migrants coming to the UK from countries badly affected by drought or sea level rise, putting pressure on services.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::2(a)": "6.5 + 2 + 4 + 0.5 + 9 + 7 + 4.5 + 3 + 2 + 10 = 48.5. 48.5 ÷ 10 = 4.85 cm per year.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::2(b)": "In the upper course the river has lots of potential energy and erodes vertically (downwards) through hydraulic action and abrasion, cutting a deep, narrow channel. This makes the valley sides steep and unstable. Weathering, such as freeze–thaw, loosens rock on the valley sides, and mass movement (e.g. soil creep) moves this material down the slopes into the river, which carries it away, so the valley develops a V shape.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::2(c)": "The River Tees in north-east England has been managed in several ways. The Tees Barrage at Stockton-on-Tees, opened in 1995, uses four hydraulic gates to control the flow of the lower river and hold back tidal surges. It has been very successful: there has been no major flooding in Stockton since it opened, protecting around 10,000 properties, and it has created a stable stretch of water used for water sports, helping regenerate the riverside with new housing and businesses.\n\nFurther upstream, the river is managed through reservoirs such as Cow Green, which regulate flow and supply water to industry and homes, and flood defences protect towns like Yarm. Flood walls and embankments protect riverside towns such as Yarm, and the Environment Agency issues flood warnings so people can prepare.\n\nHowever, management has not been completely successful. The barrage has affected fish migration, so a fish pass had to be added, and it has changed sediment movement in the estuary. Some areas can still flood during extreme rainfall, and climate change is likely to increase the risk in future, so defences may need upgrading at great cost.\n\nOverall, management of the Tees has been largely successful, especially in the lower valley where the barrage has protected thousands of homes and supported regeneration, but it has had some environmental costs and may not be enough to cope with future flood risk.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::3(a)(i)": "Plot a cross at 182 on the vertical axis directly above the year 2000, between the crosses for 1999 and 2001.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::3(a)(ii)": "Draw a single straight line of best fit rising from bottom left to top right, with roughly equal numbers of points above and below it.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::3(a)(iii)": "C — strong positive correlation.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::3(a)(iv)": "160.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::3(b)": "At about 30° north and south, air that rose at the Equator descends in the Hadley cells, creating high pressure. As the air sinks it warms, so there is no condensation and clouds cannot form. This gives very little rainfall and clear skies, creating the hot desert climatic zone.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::3(c)": "The Nepal earthquake of 25 April 2015 was caused by the collision of the Indo-Australian Plate moving north into the Eurasian Plate at a rate of about 4–5 cm a year. The plates locked together, building up huge stress, which was released when the rocks suddenly slipped along a thrust fault, producing a magnitude 7.8 earthquake.\n\nThe physical characteristics of the event made it especially hazardous. The focus was very shallow (around 8–15 km), so the energy reached the surface with little reduction, and the crust moved by about 3 m, causing intense shaking. The epicentre was only about 76–80 km from Kathmandu, a densely populated city, and the steep Himalayan terrain meant that the shaking triggered landslides and avalanches.\n\nHuman factors were equally important in turning the earthquake into a disaster that killed about 9,000 people. Many buildings were made of unreinforced brick and stone and had not been made earthquake-resistant; historic structures such as the Dharahara Tower collapsed. Poverty and remoteness meant that rescue teams could not reach many villages quickly.\n\nOverall, the plate collision was the root cause, but the shallow focus and the vulnerability of buildings and people made the event so hazardous, so the human causes of the disaster were as significant as the physical ones.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::4(a)": "The pipeline crosses the tundra, which may disrupt the migration routes of animals such as caribou.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::4(b)(i)": "C — 34 °C.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::4(b)(ii)": "About 161 mm (10 + 10 + 10 + 10 + 10 + 13 + 20 + 25 + 18 + 15 + 12 + 8).",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::4(c)": "The hot, wet climate allows plants to grow all year, so trees grow very tall to compete for sunlight. The heavy rainfall means leaves have drip tips and waxy surfaces so water runs off quickly. In return, plants affect the climate: huge amounts of water transpired by the forest rise, cool and condense, producing much of the region's rainfall.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::4(d)": "C — 20 000 m².",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::4(e)": "In Costa Rica, ecotourism has been used to manage the rainforest sustainably. At the Samasati Nature Retreat, lodges are built between the trees on stilts so no forest has to be cleared, and timber comes from local afforestation projects, protecting habitats and reducing deforestation. This is environmentally sustainable because the forest and its wildlife remain intact.\n\nIt is also economically and socially sustainable: the retreat employs local people as guides, cooks and builders, and buys local food, so the community earns a steady income. This makes the rainforest more valuable standing than cut down, giving local people a reason to protect it.\n\nCosta Rica has also used payments for ecosystem services, paying landowners to keep forest, and has protected about a quarter of its land in national parks. Forest cover has recovered from around 21% in the 1980s to over 50% today, which suggests these attempts have been successful.\n\nHowever, ecotourism operates on a small scale and can bring problems if visitor numbers grow too large, such as waste, disturbance to wildlife and the carbon emissions of long-haul flights. Overall, the attempts have been largely successful because they combine protection with income for local people, but they depend on continued tourist demand.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::5(a)": "1. Hedgerows have been removed to make bigger fields for machinery, destroying habitats for birds and insects.\n2. Heavy machinery compacts the soil, which increases surface run-off and soil erosion.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::5(b)": "A — Choosing to buy food which has been produced with minimal harm to people and the environment.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::5(c)": "1. Add a key explaining what each picture symbol represents.\n2. Add values (e.g. £ millions or tonnes) for each country, or make the symbols proportional in size to the amount imported.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::5(d)": "(98.2 ÷ 860) × 100 = 11.4%.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::5(e)": "Ethical consumerism means choosing food that causes minimal harm to people and the environment, such as Fairtrade, organic, local and seasonal food.\n\nIn many ways it is sustainable. Buying Fairtrade products guarantees farmers in poorer countries a minimum price and a social premium, so they can afford better food, education and healthcare — socially and economically sustainable. Organic farming uses fewer chemicals, protecting soils, rivers and wildlife. Buying local, seasonal food reduces food miles and the energy used to heat greenhouses, cutting carbon emissions.\n\nHowever, there are limits. Ethical products are usually more expensive, so many low-income families cannot afford them, which makes ethical consumerism hard to sustain on a large scale. Organic farming has lower yields, so more land is needed to grow the same amount of food, which could lead to habitats being cleared. Buying local food in the UK may also harm farmers in poorer countries who rely on exports.\n\nOverall, ethical consumerism is environmentally and socially sustainable for those who can afford it, but it is not yet economically sustainable for everyone, so it can only be part of the solution.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::6(a)(i)": "A table with: a header section for site number, location (grid reference), date, time and weather; a row for channel width (m); and columns for depth (cm) at 0 cm, 50 cm, 100 cm, 150 cm and so on across the channel, with a separate block for each of the three sites.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::6(a)(ii)": "B — Line graph.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::6(a)(iii)": "Plotting depth against distance across the channel as a line shows the shape of the channel cross-section, so the three sites can be compared.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p1::6(b)": "Safety — the students should choose sites where the water is shallow and slow-flowing and the banks are easy to get down, so that they can measure the channel without risk of slipping or being swept away.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::1(a)": "A — When people migrate back into inner city areas.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::1(b)": "Houses in the suburbs are usually larger, with gardens and more green space.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::1(c)": "As more people move to the suburbs, new housing estates are built on greenfield land at the edge of the city, destroying countryside and wildlife habitats. More people commute into the city by car, increasing traffic congestion and air pollution on main roads. Meanwhile, the inner city loses population and customers, so some shops and businesses close and areas can become run-down.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::1(d)(i)": "Plot a point at 24,000,000 above the year 2030 and extend the line from the 2020 point (about 21.7 million) to it.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::1(d)(ii)": "15,500,000 − 13,000,000 = 2,500,000 (2.5 million).",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::1(e)": "London is the UK's capital and most important city. Nationally, it is the seat of government and Parliament and generates around a quarter of the UK's GDP. It is the hub of the UK transport network — major railway lines and motorways radiate from it — and it has many leading universities and hospitals that people from across the country use.\n\nGlobally, London is a world city. The City of London and Canary Wharf form one of the two leading global financial centres, with hundreds of foreign banks and the London Stock Exchange, attracting investment from around the world. Heathrow is one of the world's busiest international airports, connecting London to cities across every continent. London is a major global tourist destination, attracting millions of visitors to sites such as the British Museum and the Tower of London, and is a centre of culture, media (the BBC) and fashion. Its diverse population, with over 300 languages spoken, gives it links with almost every country.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::2(a)": "B — When NGOs work in cooperation with local communities to find out about their needs.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::2(b)": "Debt relief means the government no longer has to spend large amounts on interest repayments, so it can invest that money in schools and healthcare, improving education and health.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::2(c)(i)": "A — Africa is the continent with the lowest share of the population with access to improved drinking water.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::2(c)(ii)": "A bar chart showing the average share of population with access for each continent (or proportional symbols on a map).",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::2(d)": "Zambia, in southern Africa, is an LIDC whose development has been strongly influenced by politics. Since the return to multi-party democracy in 1991, Zambia has had relatively peaceful elections and stable government, unlike some neighbours affected by civil war. This stability encouraged foreign investment and tourism, for example around Victoria Falls. In 2005–06 Zambia qualified for debt cancellation under the HIPC initiative after meeting conditions on government reform, and the government used the savings to remove fees for primary education and rural healthcare, increasing school enrolment.\n\nHowever, other factors have also been very important. Zambia's economy depends heavily on copper exports, so world copper prices have had a huge influence: when prices fell in the 1970s–90s the economy declined, and when they rose in the 2000s it grew quickly. Foreign investment, especially from China in copper mines and infrastructure, has created jobs but also debt. Zambia is landlocked, which increases transport costs, and HIV/AIDS reduced life expectancy and the workforce.\n\nOverall, politics has been a very important influence because stable government and good decisions allowed debt relief and investment in services, but I do not think it has been the greatest influence; Zambia's dependence on copper prices and foreign investment has had an even bigger effect on its rate of development.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::3(a)": "1. Falling birth rates — people are choosing to have fewer children and to have them later.\n2. Rising life expectancy because of better healthcare and healthier lifestyles.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::3(b)(i)": "C — Immigration has increased overall, but there have been some fluctuations.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::3(b)(ii)": "The UK government introduced stricter rules on immigration, such as limits on non-EU workers and students.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::3(b)(iii)": "2015.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::3(c)": "Term-time working total: 233 + 1149 = 1382. Nine days / four-and-a-half days, men: 149 − 93 = 56.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::3(d)": "UK media exports have a large global influence. British TV formats such as The X Factor are watched by around 120 million people worldwide, and 51 countries, including the USA, have made their own versions, earning money for UK companies. Children's programmes such as Peppa Pig are broadcast in over 180 countries, and dramas like Downton Abbey have huge audiences in the USA and China. British films such as the Harry Potter and James Bond series earn billions of dollars globally, and UK music artists such as Adele and Ed Sheeran top charts around the world.\n\nThis influence goes beyond money. UK media spread the English language and British culture, encouraging tourism to filming locations and boosting the UK's \"soft power\" and reputation. The BBC World Service reaches hundreds of millions of people, giving the UK influence in global news.\n\nHowever, UK media face strong competition from the USA, whose film and streaming industries are much larger, and from growing industries in India and South Korea. Overall, UK media exports have a significant global influence, especially through TV formats and English-language culture, though they are smaller than those of the USA.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p2::4": "The evidence partly supports the hypothesis. Fig. 3 shows that the congestion charge reduced traffic by 15% and congestion by 30% by 2006, and that traffic in the charging zone is now nearly a quarter lower than a decade ago. As vehicles are a major source of NO₂, this suggests pollution should have fallen.\n\nThe maps support this. In 2003 (Fig. 4), before the charge, the whole of central London and a dense web of main roads radiating in all directions were above 50 micrograms of NO₂ per cubic metre — well above the legal limit of 40. By 2016 (Fig. 5), the very high readings were confined to a much smaller central area and fewer main roads stood out, so pollution appears to have fallen both inside the charging zone and on the roads leading into it.\n\nHowever, there are limitations. Much of inner London was still above the 40 µg/m³ limit in 2016, so pollution remained a problem. The reduction may not be caused only by the congestion charge: vehicles have become cleaner due to stricter emission standards, the Low Emission Zone was introduced in 2008, more people cycle on new cycle lanes, and public transport has improved. The maps only show two years, so we cannot see the trend in between, and weather can also affect pollution levels.\n\nIn conclusion, air pollution in London has decreased since 2003 and the congestion charge has probably contributed by reducing traffic, but the evidence is not strong enough to say it is the only cause, so the hypothesis is partly accepted.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::1(a)(i)": "Milton Keynes is in south-east England, north-west of London and south-east of Birmingham, next to the M1 motorway.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::1(a)(ii)": "About 80 km.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::1(b)(i)": "0.6% (0.2% male + 0.4% female).",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::1(b)(ii)": "Milton Keynes: 3.4 + 3.7 + 4.1 + 4.1 = 15.3%. England: 3.4 + 3.4 + 3.3 + 3.3 = 13.4%. Difference = 15.3 − 13.4 = 1.9%.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::1(c)": "The two pyramids are similar in some ways. Both narrow towards the top, showing that both populations are ageing and only a small percentage of people are over 80 (2.0% of men in Milton Keynes and 3.0% in England). The proportions aged 45–64 are also quite similar.\n\nHowever, there are important differences. Milton Keynes has a younger population: it has more young children (0–4 is 6.9% compared with 5.9% in England, and 5–9 is 7.7% compared with 6.2%) and many more adults aged 30–44 (35–39 is 8.2% compared with 6.6%). This is linked — young adults who have moved to Milton Keynes for jobs and housing are having children, which raises the birth rate. Milton Keynes has fewer people aged 15–24 (20–24 is 4.4% compared with 6.0%), probably because young people leave for university, and fewer elderly people (70–74 is 3.9% compared with 5.0%), because Milton Keynes is a newer town that attracted working families rather than retirees.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::2(a)": "1. There are lots of jobs — more were created there in the last decade than anywhere else in the UK.\n2. There are lots of parks and green spaces, making it a healthy place to live.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::2(b)(i)": "In 1960 the built-up areas were small and dispersed, consisting mainly of separate villages such as Woughton on the Green rather than one town. There were more built-up areas in the north-west of the area, and the villages tended to follow the roads and rivers. The rest of the area was countryside.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::2(b)(ii)": "Urbanisation is an increase in the proportion of people living in towns and cities. It happens when people move from rural areas to urban areas, attracted by more jobs and better services, and through natural increase, which means large amounts of new housing are built — as in Milton Keynes, where scattered villages became a city.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::2(c)": "Milton Keynes grew from 15 small villages in 1960 into a large city for both economic and social reasons.\n\nThe most important economic reason was government policy. After the Second World War, London was overcrowded, so the government planned New Towns to provide housing and workspace outside London; Milton Keynes was the largest, designed for 250,000 people (Fig. 4). Its location was ideal: it lies beside the M1, opened in 1959 as the UK's first motorway, about 80 km north-west of London and between London and Birmingham (Fig. 1). This good accessibility encouraged businesses to set up there, because they could transport goods easily and attract workers. Today Milton Keynes has excellent office facilities and a skilled workforce, and more jobs were created there over the last decade than anywhere else in the UK (Fig. 3), which creates a multiplier effect, attracting more businesses and people.\n\nSocial reasons also attracted people. The city was designed to be a pleasant place to live: 25% of it is green space, with parks and playgrounds in every residential area and 270 km of redways for walking and cycling, so the personal trainer says it is a great place to stay healthy. The 15 villages incorporated into the plan give a friendly \"village within a city\" feel that appeals to families. The grid road system with roundabouts was designed to reduce congestion, and water-storage lakes reduce flood risk (Fig. 5). As a result, Milton Keynes has attracted many young families: Fig. 2 shows it has more people aged 30–44 and more children aged 0–14 than England as a whole, which in turn has added to growth through natural increase.\n\nThese reasons are linked to ideas from elsewhere in the course: like other cities in ACs, Milton Keynes has grown through counter-urbanisation from London and through the growth of the tertiary sector, supported by good transport links. Overall, government planning and its excellent location were the main causes of growth, but the high quality of life has kept people and businesses coming.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::3(a)": "People burn fossil fuels such as petrol in cars and coal in power stations, which releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere; this traps more heat, enhancing the greenhouse effect.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::3(b)(i)": "Triple the number of electric cars and buses.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::3(b)(ii)": "188,500 + 214,500 + 25,000 + 136,000 + 265,000 + 171,000 = £1,000,000.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::3(c)": "The plans could help reduce the enhanced greenhouse effect in several ways. Planting more trees on the 25% of the city that is open space would absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere through photosynthesis, increasing the carbon sink. Installing electric charging points and tripling the number of electric cars and buses would reduce emissions from petrol and diesel vehicles, especially if the electricity comes from the new solar panels on council buildings rather than fossil fuels. Encouraging people to walk and cycle on the redways, and making public transport easier with a mobile phone app, would reduce the number of car journeys and so cut CO₂ emissions.\n\nHowever, the impact may be limited. Fewer than 1% of cars are currently electric, so even tripling the number would still leave almost all cars running on fossil fuels. Over 1,500 houses are being built on green space every year, removing vegetation that stores carbon, and the city was designed around cars, so people may not change their habits. Overall, the plans should help reduce emissions, but they would need to go further to make Milton Keynes carbon negative by 2050.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2022-p3::4": "Environmental sustainability means meeting the needs of people today without damaging the environment for future generations.\n\nIn many ways Milton Keynes is environmentally sustainable. A quarter of the city is green space, with parks in every residential area, which provides habitats for wildlife and absorbs carbon dioxide. There are 270 km of redways that let people walk and cycle instead of driving, reducing air pollution and emissions. 75% of household rubbish is recycled, reducing waste sent to landfill. The water-storage lakes reduce flood risk by working with the river rather than relying on hard engineering, and the grid road system was designed to keep traffic moving and reduce congestion. The council has a vision to be carbon negative by 2050, with plans for more trees, solar panels and electric buses.\n\nHowever, there are important ways in which it is not sustainable. The city was designed around the car: fewer than 1% of cars are electric, the young family complains of traffic at roundabouts, and the 22-year-old says buses are so slow they use taxis, so transport emissions remain high. Over 1,500 houses are built on green space every year, reducing the green areas that make the city attractive and store carbon, and as the population grows, energy and water use will increase. Many of the green plans are still only proposals.\n\nOverall, Milton Keynes is more environmentally sustainable than many UK cities because of its green spaces, redways and recycling, but its dependence on cars and its continued expansion onto green land mean it is not yet fully sustainable.\n\nOne additional way Milton Keynes could become more sustainable is to create dedicated bus lanes along the grid roads. This would make buses faster and more reliable than cars, solving the problem described by the 22-year-old, so more people would use public transport instead of driving. Combined with the extra electric buses, this would significantly reduce carbon emissions and air pollution, and it would also reduce congestion at roundabouts, making the city more pleasant to live in.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::1(a)(i)": "D — plates move towards each other.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::1(a)(ii)": "Using the scale, draw a curved line 7 km from La Soufrière around the volcano across the island, and shade the band between Zone 1 (4 km) and this line with the crosses for Zone 2 (high risk). Then draw a second curved line 10 km from the volcano and shade the band between 7 km and 10 km with the dashed shading for Zone 3 (moderate risk).",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::1(b)(i)": "Day 1: plot a cross at 59°W, 13°N. Day 2: plot a cross at 64°W, 17°N (both south-east of Day 3).",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::1(b)(ii)": "Join the points together and label every day, so the track and the hurricane's speed can be seen clearly.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::1(c)": "Named hazard: Typhoon Haiyan, Philippines, November 2013.\n\nHaiyan was one of the strongest tropical storms ever recorded, with winds of over 300 km/h and a storm surge of over 5 m. The primary consequences were devastating: over 6,000 people were killed, mostly by the storm surge in Tacloban on Leyte, where 90% of the city was destroyed. Around 1 million homes were damaged or destroyed and about 4 million people were made homeless.\n\nThe secondary consequences were also severe. Water supplies were contaminated and power was cut off, leading to outbreaks of disease. Roads and the airport at Tacloban were blocked, so aid was slow to arrive, and looting broke out as people became desperate for food. Around 6 million workers lost their livelihoods because crops such as coconuts and rice and fishing boats were destroyed.\n\nThe disaster led to a large international response: countries such as the UK and USA sent aid and military help, and NGOs provided shelter and water purification. In the long term, the government launched a rebuilding plan with stronger homes and a \"no-build zone\" along the coast, although many people were reluctant to move away from their livelihoods. Many people migrated to Manila and other cities in search of work.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::2(a)": "As global temperatures have risen, the oceans have warmed and expanded (thermal expansion), and land ice such as glaciers and ice sheets has melted, adding water to the oceans — both cause mean sea level to rise, as shown by the increase of about 190 mm since 1900.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::2(b)(i)": "A — frozen ocean water.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::2(b)(ii)": "32,235 − 24,097 = 8,138 km³ lost over 30 years. 8,138 ÷ 30 = 271.27 km³ per year.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::2(c)": "Climate change has always happened naturally. Milankovitch cycles — changes in the Earth's orbit, tilt and wobble — alter the amount of solar energy reaching the Earth over tens of thousands of years and explain the glacial and interglacial periods of the Quaternary. Sunspot activity changes solar output, and periods of low activity, such as the Maunder Minimum, coincided with the Little Ice Age. Large volcanic eruptions such as Pinatubo in 1991 released ash and sulphur dioxide, cooling global temperatures by about 0.5 °C for a year or two.\n\nHowever, these natural processes cannot explain recent warming. Milankovitch cycles act over very long periods and have changed little in the last 150 years, and solar output has not increased in recent decades. Since the Industrial Revolution, human activities have greatly enhanced the natural greenhouse effect: burning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide, deforestation removes carbon sinks, and cattle farming, rice paddies and landfill release methane. Carbon dioxide levels have risen from about 280 ppm to over 410 ppm, and temperatures have risen by about 0.18 °C per decade since 1981, far faster than natural changes.\n\nTherefore, climate change is a natural process over long timescales, and the natural greenhouse effect is essential for life. But the rapid change happening today is mainly caused by humans. I judge that climate change is only partly natural: natural processes set the background, but current warming is largely the result of the human-enhanced greenhouse effect.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::3(a)": "A — everything that can be seen that is natural or man-made.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::3(b)(i)": "Similarity: both upland areas and glaciated areas are mainly in the north and west of the UK. Difference: there are upland areas in south-west England (Dartmoor and Exmoor) that were not glaciated, whereas the glaciated area also extends across lowland areas to the coast.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::3(b)(ii)": "The north of the UK is at a higher latitude and has higher altitude, so it was colder, allowing ice sheets to form, while the south was too warm.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::3(c)": "When a river floods, it overtops its banks and spreads across the floodplain. As it leaves the channel, the water slows down and loses energy, so it deposits sediment. The heaviest, coarsest sediment is deposited first, right next to the channel, and finer material further away. Repeated floods build up this coarse material into raised banks called levees.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::3(d)": "Named coastal landscape: the Dorset coast. Landform: Old Harry stack.\n\nOld Harry is a stack at the end of the Foreland, a headland made of resistant chalk on a discordant coastline, where softer rocks have been eroded to form Studland Bay and Swanage Bay. Because the headland sticks out into the sea, it is exposed to powerful waves, and wave refraction concentrates their energy on it. Hydraulic action forces air into joints and faults in the chalk, compressing it and widening the cracks, while abrasion by pebbles thrown against the cliff erodes them further. Over time the cracks are enlarged into caves.\n\nIf caves form on both sides of the headland, they are eroded until they break through, forming an arch, such as the one that once linked Old Harry to the mainland. Weathering of the arch roof — chemical weathering by slightly acidic rainwater dissolving the chalk, and freeze-thaw — combined with continued erosion at the base, weakens it until the roof collapses under its own weight, leaving Old Harry as an isolated stack. The base of the stack continues to be undercut, and eventually it collapses to form a stump, as happened to Old Harry's Wife in 1896.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::4(a)(i)": "June.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::4(a)(ii)": "(24 × 30) + (23 × 31) + (22 × 31) = 720 + 713 + 682 = 2,115 hours.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::4(a)(iii)": "A — line graph.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::4(b)": "1. Plants are low-growing and compact, such as cushion plants, which protects them from the strong, cold winds and keeps them under the snow, where it is warmer.\n2. They have shallow roots, because the ground below is permanently frozen (permafrost), so roots can only grow in the thin active layer.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::4(c)": "Named example: the Clyde River bowhead whale sanctuary, Nunavut, Arctic Canada.\n\nIn 2008 a marine wildlife area was created at Isabella Bay near Clyde River, following a campaign by the local Inuit community. It protects an important feeding and resting area used by around 2,000 bowhead whales each year. Commercial whaling had greatly reduced the bowhead population, so the sanctuary is useful in helping it recover by limiting disturbance from shipping and development.\n\nThe scheme is sustainable because it was led by local people: the Inuit helped monitor the whales and benefit from low-impact eco-tourism, so conservation brings them income while protecting their culture and environment for future generations. Its small scale makes it easy to manage and enforce.\n\nHowever, its usefulness is limited. The whales are migratory, so they are only protected while in the sanctuary and remain at risk elsewhere from shipping, pollution and climate change, which is reducing sea ice. A small sanctuary cannot address these wider threats. Overall, small-scale management is useful as it is locally supported and effective in protecting a key habitat, but it needs to be combined with larger-scale agreements to provide a fully sustainable solution for the Arctic.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::5(a)": "I used a scatter graph to show the relationship between distance downstream and river width, because it clearly showed the trend and allowed me to add a line of best fit and spot anomalies.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::5(b)(i)": "Place A: 10 ÷ 25.3 = 0.4 m/s. Place B: 10 ÷ 33.0 = 0.3 m/s.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::5(b)(ii)": "(38 + 32 + 33) ÷ 3 = 34.3 seconds.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::5(b)(iii)": "1. The river flows fastest at place A on the outside of the bend (0.4 m/s).\n2. Attempt 1 at place C (38 seconds) is an anomaly, as it is much slower than the other two attempts.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p1::5(b)(iv)": "The method has several weaknesses. The orange is carried by the current, so on a bend it is likely to drift out of its \"lane\" towards the outside of the meander; this means the time recorded at places B and C may not reflect the speed at that point. An orange also only measures surface velocity, which is affected by wind, whereas the speed varies with depth. Timing with a stopwatch involves human reaction error, and only three attempts were made, so one anomaly — such as 38 seconds at C — has a large effect on the mean.\n\nThe method could be improved by using a flowmeter, which measures velocity precisely at a fixed point and at different depths, removing the problem of drifting. If floats are still used, more repeats (for example five or ten at each place) would make the mean more reliable and reduce the effect of anomalies. Measurements should be taken at more points across the channel (for example every metre) to show the pattern more accurately, and the same person should time each run to reduce variation in reaction time.\n\nFinally, the investigation only looked at one meander on one day. Repeating it at other meanders and in different flow conditions, such as after heavy rain, would show whether the results are representative. Overall, using a flowmeter at more points would make the biggest improvement to accuracy, while more repeats and more sites would improve reliability.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::1(a)": "B — An increase in the proportion of people living in towns and cities.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::1(b)(i)": "Proportional symbols on a world map, with circles sized according to each city's population.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::1(b)(ii)": "32,869 − 25,582 = 7,287. 7,287 ÷ 25,582 × 100 = 28.5%.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::1(b)(iii)": "Cities grow faster than housing can be built, so people build their own homes in informal settlements on unsafe land. These homes are crammed together without proper sanitation, so diseases such as cholera spread easily. There are not enough formal jobs, so many people work in the informal sector with low, unreliable pay.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::1(c)": "Named city: Lagos, Nigeria (an LIDC).\n\nWithin its region, Lagos is the largest city in Nigeria, with over 10% of the country's population. It provides jobs, schools and hospitals for people from the surrounding area, and its growth has attracted millions of rural–urban migrants from across Nigeria.\n\nWithin the country, Lagos is Nigeria's commercial and financial centre. Around 80% of Nigeria's industry is based in or near the city, and it produces about 30% of the country's GDP. Its port handles most of Nigeria's imports, and the main television and radio stations are based there, so it shapes national culture.\n\nIn the wider world, Lagos is the main financial centre for West Africa, and around 80% of flights to West Africa use its Murtala Muhammed International Airport, making it a gateway for international business and tourism. It is home to the Nigerian Stock Exchange and Nollywood, the world's second-largest film industry by output, which exports films across Africa and beyond.\n\nOverall, Lagos is most important at a national scale, as the engine of Nigeria's economy, but its role in West Africa makes it increasingly important globally.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::2(a)(i)": "Plot a cross where 99% on the literacy (x) axis meets 79 years on the life expectancy (y) axis.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::2(a)(ii)": "Draw a straight, ruled line rising from left to right through the middle of the points, with roughly equal numbers of points above and below it.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::2(a)(iii)": "C — There is a positive correlation.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::2(b)": "A country in debt must use much of its income to repay the loans and interest, so it has less money to spend on education, healthcare and infrastructure, which are needed for development.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::2(c)": "Named LIDC: Zambia.\n\nZambia's most important global connection is trade in copper, which makes up about 70% of its exports. China is the world's largest consumer of copper, and around 500 Chinese companies have invested in Zambia. For example, the China Non-Ferrous Metal company bought the Chambishi copper mine and invested in improving it, creating jobs and training workers in new mining and manufacturing techniques. Chinese investment has also funded new roads, railways and telecommunications, which help other businesses grow. These connections have increased Zambia's GDP and helped it develop.\n\nHowever, these connections also have drawbacks. Relying on one export makes Zambia vulnerable to changes in world copper prices; when prices fell, mines closed and jobs were lost. Much of the profit goes to foreign companies rather than staying in Zambia, and there have been concerns about poor working conditions and safety in Chinese-owned mines. Zambia also borrowed heavily from China, leading to debt problems.\n\nOther global connections have influenced development too. Aid from organisations such as the UK government and NGOs has supported healthcare, including HIV/AIDS treatment, and tourism to Victoria Falls brings in foreign currency.\n\nOverall, global connections — especially trade and Chinese investment — have had a large influence on Zambia's development, improving infrastructure and income. However, because of dependence on copper and debt, they have not led to development that benefits everyone, so internal factors such as government policy and education also matter.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::3(a)(i)": "A — Areas with the highest population density are around cities.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::3(a)(ii)": "Label the main cities on the map so the patterns can be identified.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::3(b)(i)": "2.7 + 2.2 + 1.8 + 1.3 + 1.2 = 9.2 million.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::3(b)(ii)": "Older people are more likely to suffer from long-term illnesses, so they need more hospital care, which increases costs for the NHS. Many older people need care, but there are too few care homes, so middle-aged children look after them, reducing the size of the workforce. However, many retired people volunteer in their communities and spend money on leisure, which supports the economy.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::3(c)": "One major change was the 2008 recession, when many businesses closed and unemployment rose from about 1.6 million in 2008 to 2.7 million by 2011. This reduced incomes and spending, and many people took on part-time work or zero-hours contracts; these give companies flexibility, but workers have no guaranteed hours and therefore an unstable income.\n\nThe job market has continued to shift from manufacturing to services. Most new jobs are in the tertiary and quaternary sectors, such as finance, IT and research, which are concentrated in London and the South East, widening the economic gap with northern regions.\n\nThe Covid-19 pandemic in 2020 had a major impact: hospitality and retail were closed, and the government's furlough scheme paid up to 80% of wages to prevent mass unemployment. Many office workers moved to working from home, which has continued, reducing spending in city centres but supporting local economies elsewhere. Brexit has also reduced the number of EU workers, creating labour shortages in sectors such as farming, haulage and hospitality, which has pushed up wages in some industries.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::4(a)(i)": "A — When all people, at all times, have access to sufficient, safe and nutritious food.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::4(a)(ii)": "War and conflict reduce food security because farmers are forced to flee their land, so crops are not planted or harvested, and fighting disrupts transport routes, so food cannot reach markets.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::4(b)(i)": "1. The percentage for each type of food.\n2. The year the data was collected.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::4(b)(ii)": "It makes people aware of how much food is wasted, so they may buy only what they need and plan their meals.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::4(c)": "Changes in food production methods can make food production more sustainable in different ways. Organic farming avoids chemical pesticides and fertilisers, so more insect species, including pollinators, survive, and soils are protected; animals are given more space and fewer antibiotics. This is environmentally sustainable, but organic farming produces lower yields and its food is more expensive, so it may not be economically sustainable or feed a growing population.\n\nHydroponics grows crops in nutrient-rich water indoors. It uses up to 90% less water than traditional farming and can be done in cities, reducing food miles, but it needs a lot of energy for lighting and heating, which can make it less sustainable unless renewable energy is used.\n\nUrban gardens and allotments allow people to grow their own food locally, reducing transport emissions and improving diets and community ties, although they produce only small amounts.\n\nBy contrast, intensive farming and the Green Revolution greatly increased yields, which improved food security, but heavy use of fertilisers, pesticides and irrigation has caused water pollution, soil degradation and loss of biodiversity, so they are less environmentally sustainable. Overall, new methods can make food production more sustainable, but each has trade-offs between environmental benefits and the amount of food produced.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::5(a)(i)": "1. A north arrow.\n2. A scale.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::5(a)(ii)": "D — Traffic count.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::5(a)(iii)": "A traffic count shows how busy the roads are, which indicates levels of noise and air pollution from vehicles; repeating it after the estate is built would show the increase in traffic it causes.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::5(b)": "I used a bar chart to show the environmental quality scores at each site; it made it easy to compare the sites visually and see which areas had the lowest scores.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p2::5(c)": "Our enquiry investigated whether environmental quality improved with distance from the town centre. We used an environmental quality survey, scoring factors such as litter, noise and building condition from −3 to +3 at ten sites along a transect, and a questionnaire asking residents about their area.\n\nThe environmental quality survey was partly successful. It produced numerical data that could be compared between sites and plotted against distance, and it showed a general increase in scores away from the centre, supporting our hypothesis. However, the scores were subjective — different students judged \"litter\" differently — so the results may not be accurate. We visited each site only once, on a weekday afternoon, so noise and traffic may not have been typical.\n\nThe questionnaire gave useful qualitative data about how residents felt, which helped explain our results. However, we only asked 20 people, mainly those who were willing to stop, so the sample may not have been representative, and older people were over-represented.\n\nOverall, our primary data allowed us to reach a conclusion that environmental quality generally improves away from the centre, but the conclusion is only partly evidenced because of the subjectivity of the survey, the single visit and the small sample. Using a group average for each score, visiting at different times and using systematic sampling for the questionnaire would make our conclusions more reliable.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::1(a)": "Mexico City is in the south of Mexico, about 500 km east-south-east of Guadalajara and about 300 km north-east of Acapulco.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::1(b)(i)": "127.6 − 67.5 = 60.1 million.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::1(b)(ii)": "66.6 : 33.4, which is about 2:1.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::1(b)(iii)": "1. Mexico's GNI per capita (US$19,160) is less than half that of the UK (US$46,071) but much higher than an LIDC, because Mexico is industrialising and creating better-paid manufacturing and service jobs.\n2. Mexico's fertility rate (2.13) is higher than the UK's (1.68) but lower than in LIDCs, because more women are educated and have access to contraception, so families are getting smaller as the country develops.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::1(c)": "Mexico City has a distinct wet season and dry season. Precipitation is very low from November to February, with the lowest total of 6 mm in December. It increases from March, rising sharply in May (54 mm) to a peak of 124 mm in July, and stays above 100 mm from June to September before falling rapidly in October (52 mm) and November (8 mm). The annual range is 118 mm.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::1(d)": "People in Mexico City face serious issues with water supply. Precipitation is very seasonal: from November to February it is under 12 mm a month, so there is little rainfall to replenish supplies in the dry season, while 40% of the city's clean water comes from underground reservoirs that are drying up through overuse. As about 20% of Mexico's population live in the city, demand for water is enormous, so shortages are likely, particularly in poorer informal areas, where water already has to be delivered by truck.\n\nThe infrastructure makes this worse. About 40% of clean water is lost through leaks, and old, poorly maintained sewage and water pipes often burst. This means less clean water reaches homes and there is a risk of sewage contaminating drinking water, spreading diseases such as cholera and diarrhoea, which spread quickly in overcrowded settlements and put pressure on healthcare.\n\nIn contrast, during the wet season from June to September, over 100 mm falls each month, and climate change is causing more extreme rainfall events. The city produces 40,000 litres of sewage every second, so heavy rain can overwhelm the drains, causing flooding and sewage overflows. So people may face both too little water in the dry season and contaminated floodwater in the wet season — a problem similar to that of many rapidly growing cities in LIDCs and EDCs.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::2(a)(i)": "1%.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::2(a)(ii)": "Landslides and flooding.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::2(a)(iii)": "Polanco has lots of trees and green space, whereas Neza-Chalco-Itza is densely packed with houses and has very little greenery.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::2(b)": "Large numbers of people migrate from rural areas to cities in search of work, so the population grows faster than formal housing can be built. Many migrants are poor and cannot afford to rent or buy a home, and the government does not have enough money to provide housing for everyone. As a result, people build their own homes on unused land, often on steep slopes or floodplains, forming informal settlements.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::2(c)": "Mexico City is a highly unequal city, and this inequality causes many of its contemporary challenges. Just 1% of Mexico's population earns 21% of national income, and much of this wealth is concentrated in Polanco in the west, while over 4 million people live in Neza-Chalco-Itza in the east, one of the largest informal settlements in the world.\n\nThe biggest challenge is housing. In Neza-Chalco-Itza, houses are poorly built, close together and lack planning regulations, often on steep or swampy land. This exposes the poorest residents to the greatest risks: the authorities warn that landslides and flooding may increase with climate change, especially as rainfall reaches 124 mm in July. In Polanco, by contrast, people live in modern, well-built apartments surrounded by landscaped parks, so they face far lower risks.\n\nInequality also affects access to water and services. Across the city, 40% of clean water is lost through leaking pipes and the underground reservoirs are drying up, but the shortage is felt most by the poor: in parts of Neza-Chalco-Itza, water is delivered by truck and carried by donkey twice a week, whereas wealthy areas have reliable supplies. Poor sanitation in crowded informal areas increases the risk of disease.\n\nOpportunities are unequal too. Polanco has the best schools and hospitals in Mexico and modern offices, while many people in the east do informal or low-paid jobs with no security. This limits education and income, so the cycle of poverty continues and the gap widens.\n\nNot all challenges are caused by inequality: water shortages and hazards affect the whole city because of its large population (21.8 million) and physical setting. However, inequality determines who suffers most. Overall, I think inequality is the main cause of the most serious challenges, because it forces millions of people to live in unsafe, poorly serviced settlements where the city's wider problems hit hardest.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::3(a)(i)": "11.65 + 5.93 = US$17.58 billion.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::3(a)(ii)": "Earthquakes can cause buildings to collapse, which can kill or injure people who are trapped inside, and can make many people homeless.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::3(b)": "Tectonic hazards have serious consequences for people in Mexico City. The 1985 magnitude 8 earthquake killed at least 5,000 people, and many houses in informal settlements collapsed because they were poorly built without foundations. The effects were made worse because the city is built on a drained lake bed, which amplified the seismic waves. As another large earthquake is very likely, the millions of people in informal settlements are at high risk of death, injury and homelessness, and the government may not have enough money to rebuild.\n\nPopocatépetl, only 70 km away, has been regularly active since 1994. Ash clouds reaching the city can cause breathing problems, damage crops and close airports, disrupting the economy. If ash mixes with heavy rain, mudflows could form on steep slopes, where many informal settlements are built.\n\nThe economic consequences are also large: US$17.58 billion of the city's GDP — 29% — is at risk from volcanoes and earthquakes, money that would be needed to rebuild rather than spent on development. Overall, earthquakes are the greatest threat to life, while the poorest people suffer most because their housing is least able to withstand hazards.",
  "geog-ocr-j384-2023-p3::4": "Mexico City faces many contemporary challenges, including water shortages, flooding, tectonic hazards, informal settlements and inequality. Some of these are caused by physical factors. The climate is highly seasonal, with very little rain from November to February and over 100 mm a month in summer, so there are both shortages and floods. Its location on the North American Plate near the Cocos Plate means earthquakes are common, as in 1985 when at least 5,000 people died, and Popocatépetl, 70 km away, regularly erupts. The city is built on a drained lake bed that amplifies shaking, and many settlements are on steep or swampy land at risk of landslides and flooding.\n\nHowever, human factors are arguably more important. The population has grown to 21.8 million — about 20% of Mexico's population — through rural–urban migration, which has increased demand for water so much that underground reservoirs are drying up. Poor management means 40% of clean water is lost through leaking pipes. Inequality means the poorest people, such as the 4 million in Neza-Chalco-Itza, live in poorly built informal housing with water delivered by truck, while Polanco has modern homes and the best services. Physical hazards only become disasters because of these human factors: the 1985 earthquake killed so many because poorly built homes collapsed.\n\nOverall, I judge that physical factors create the risks, but human factors — rapid growth, poor infrastructure and inequality — are the main cause of the challenges residents face, because they determine who is exposed and how vulnerable they are.\n\nOne initiative that could support long-term sustainability is a city-wide programme to repair the water network combined with rainwater harvesting. Fixing leaks would recover much of the 40% of water lost, reducing the need to pump from overused aquifers. Collecting rainwater from roofs during the wet season, when over 100 mm falls each month, would provide water for the dry season and reduce flooding by slowing run-off. If targeted first at informal settlements such as Neza-Chalco-Itza, it would reduce inequality and disease, improving health. This is environmentally sustainable because it allows aquifers to recover, socially sustainable because it gives the poorest people reliable water, and economically sustainable because rainwater tanks are cheap and can be installed by local communities, as in self-help schemes in other cities such as Rio de Janeiro.",
};
